1
00:00:42,902 --> 00:00:45,772
MAN: Stop it! Alan, stop!

2
00:00:46,472 --> 00:00:48,342
(SHOUTING)

3
00:00:56,882 --> 00:00:58,582
(RINGING DOORBELL)

4
00:01:00,452 --> 00:01:04,335
- Donald. Donald, are you all right?
- DONALD: Stop it! Stop, Alan!

5
00:01:13,062 --> 00:01:14,112
Donald?

6
00:01:19,702 --> 00:01:20,702
(WOMAN GASPS)

7
00:01:51,572 --> 00:01:53,712
Do you want to turn your engine off?

8
00:01:55,672 --> 00:01:58,571
- Do you have some identity?
- Is there something wrong?

9
00:01:58,572 --> 00:02:00,311
Where have you just come from, sir?

10
00:02:00,312 --> 00:02:02,181
Would you mind telling me
what this is all about?

11
00:02:02,182 --> 00:02:03,888
Would you get out of the car?

12
00:02:04,082 --> 00:02:06,981
Look, I'm an MP.
I'm Alan Roxborough. What is this?

13
00:02:06,982 --> 00:02:08,092
Now, sir.

14
00:02:15,022 --> 00:02:18,772
- Do you know a Donald Toombs?
- Um, no.

15
00:02:19,492 --> 00:02:22,901
No, I don't think I do. Is he
a constituent? I am in rather a hurry.

16
00:02:22,902 --> 00:02:26,001
We'd like you to accompany us
to the police station, sir.

17
00:02:26,002 --> 00:02:27,826
What for? Are you arresting me?

18
00:02:28,072 --> 00:02:30,411
- Have you done something wrong?
- No, of course not.

19
00:02:30,412 --> 00:02:32,342
Well, that's all right, then.

20
00:02:56,102 --> 00:02:57,712
(WHISPERING)

21
00:03:00,272 --> 00:03:01,920
DEED: There's no difference.

22
00:03:01,972 --> 00:03:05,452
Not that I can see,
nor any the expert witness pointed out.

23
00:03:06,342 --> 00:03:07,482
Thank you.

24
00:03:11,782 --> 00:03:15,665
If a customer went into one of your
client's many high-street shops

25
00:03:16,052 --> 00:03:19,464
and stole a jumper, Mrs Channing,
they'd rightly prosecute.

26
00:03:19,922 --> 00:03:23,082
What your clients have done
is tantamount to theft.

27
00:03:23,932 --> 00:03:26,261
They went into this struggling
young designer's shop,

28
00:03:26,262 --> 00:03:28,931
purchased a set of her
highly individual underwear

29
00:03:28,932 --> 00:03:31,638
and then copied them
for their own store label.

30
00:03:32,272 --> 00:03:35,567
I'm therefore giving judgment
in favour of the claimant.

31
00:03:36,102 --> 00:03:39,985
With your leave, My Lord, the defendants
seek permission to appeal.

32
00:03:40,312 --> 00:03:44,442
No. The application is refused.
They will pay indemnity costs.

33
00:03:45,082 --> 00:03:47,632
These will be in the region of ￡100,000.

34
00:03:48,052 --> 00:03:51,212
And aggravated damages of ￡300,000.

35
00:03:52,922 --> 00:03:54,262
How surprising.

36
00:03:54,692 --> 00:03:57,045
Yeah, what I would prefer, Mrs Channing,

37
00:03:57,162 --> 00:04:00,061
is the directors of the company
to pay this personally

38
00:04:00,062 --> 00:04:03,601
- from their salaries and dividends.
- My Lord, you cannot do that.

39
00:04:03,602 --> 00:04:05,931
The directors are protected
by limited liability.

40
00:04:05,932 --> 00:04:07,362
Yes. What a pity.

41
00:04:08,472 --> 00:04:11,237
And perhaps they should
be prosecuted for theft.

42
00:04:14,291 --> 00:04:17,260
What makes you so uneasy
about the MP going for trial?

43
00:04:17,261 --> 00:04:21,026
- The inconsistencies in the evidence.
- Did you talk to the CPS?

44
00:04:22,230 --> 00:04:26,130
I think there's a hidden agenda.
Someone is pushing for the trial.

45
00:04:27,501 --> 00:04:31,031
Like who? I thought Roxborough
was on the side of the angels.

46
00:04:31,070 --> 00:04:33,709
Well, it's not the angels
who want him stopped.

47
00:04:33,710 --> 00:04:36,749
Roxborough has been asking questions
about unexplained deaths

48
00:04:36,750 --> 00:04:38,280
in the armaments industry.

49
00:04:38,350 --> 00:04:41,779
Now, it's causing embarrassment
in certain government quarters.

50
00:04:41,780 --> 00:04:44,957
Being charged with attempted murder
could be connected.

51
00:04:45,350 --> 00:04:47,810
I'm not sure
we should be discussing this.

52
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:50,208
I could be hearing the case.

53
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:52,690
Well, I'd feel a lot easier if you were.

54
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:02,393
Coop, who's got
the Alan Roxborough case?

55
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:04,650
Mr Justice Everard, Judge.

56
00:05:05,671 --> 00:05:08,539
Mrs Mills phoned and asked
if you were free at lunchtime.

57
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:10,470
I am. Did you tell her I was?

58
00:05:19,390 --> 00:05:22,789
I left you four messages about coming
with me to the Presider's party.

59
00:05:22,790 --> 00:05:24,379
Not such a good idea, John.

60
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:28,502
The clerks in chambers have finally
remembered that I'm still alive.

61
00:05:28,660 --> 00:05:30,499
- They've found you some work?
- Mmm.

62
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:32,670
Prosecuting the MP Alan Roxborough.

63
00:05:33,030 --> 00:05:37,383
The CPS changed their mind and decided
that I was the right lead after all.

64
00:05:37,411 --> 00:05:38,411
Good.

65
00:05:39,511 --> 00:05:40,851
Who's for the defence?

66
00:05:41,110 --> 00:05:43,190
- Brian Cantwell.
- Ah.

67
00:05:46,651 --> 00:05:50,534
Row Colemore thought there might
be something wrong with that case.

68
00:05:52,090 --> 00:05:53,490
Did he say what?

69
00:05:54,590 --> 00:05:57,550
Not really.
You should talk to him yourself.

70
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:03,469
The evidence is straightforward.
I've no doubt about his guilt.

71
00:06:03,470 --> 00:06:07,839
Roxborough was having an affair with a
young man who tried blackmailing him.

72
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:10,605
Roxborough was about
to get a job in government.

73
00:06:13,710 --> 00:06:16,460
So will you come with me
to Legover's party?

74
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:19,904
I don't think I want to
remind Mr Justice Everard

75
00:06:19,980 --> 00:06:22,000
that we even know one another.

76
00:06:24,620 --> 00:06:27,209
- How's it going with your therapist?
- Good.

77
00:06:28,290 --> 00:06:32,467
- I haven't propositioned her.
- You must be coming down with something.

78
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:38,184
MAN: It seems to me to be
increasing the problem.

79
00:06:38,600 --> 00:06:41,030
I'd flog them. Flog them all.

80
00:06:41,470 --> 00:06:45,160
Or are we to be held hostage
to these guttersnipes?

81
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:50,410
It won't get him a promotion.

82
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:52,660
- Excuse me. Hi.
- Of course.

83
00:06:53,380 --> 00:06:55,322
I thought you were cross with me.

84
00:06:55,380 --> 00:06:58,380
- No Little Miss Oxfam?
- We're not an item, George.

85
00:06:58,550 --> 00:07:01,010
Oh. Isn't your psychotherapy working?

86
00:07:02,620 --> 00:07:05,059
Why don't I just put
a notice in The Times?

87
00:07:05,060 --> 00:07:06,960
I think Charlie already did.

88
00:07:07,830 --> 00:07:10,960
I always knew you couldn't form
real relationships.

89
00:07:11,570 --> 00:07:13,590
You should talk to my therapist.

90
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:17,239
I think lover-boy is trying
to get your attention.

91
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:21,123
Oh, he probably wants to be rescued.
Have you met Sir Monty's wife?

92
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:23,530
She's terribly fierce.

93
00:07:23,850 --> 00:07:27,556
She likes to join him on the bench
for his more salacious cases.

94
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:30,819
I thought you were giving up
your cabinet minister.

95
00:07:30,820 --> 00:07:34,468
Well, it's a bit like smoking, John.
The habit is hard to kick.

96
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:37,189
Perhaps you should see my therapist.

97
00:07:37,190 --> 00:07:40,661
I thought I'd never get away.
John, how's the therapy going?

98
00:07:42,730 --> 00:07:44,799
I really am gonna have to have
a word with Charlie.

99
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:47,439
Oh, half the cabinet's
seen a psychotherapist at some point.

100
00:07:47,440 --> 00:07:48,852
And the other half will.

101
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:51,982
I don't think psychics
fall into the same category.

102
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:56,909
Were you setting Monty Everard straight
on the Alan Roxborough trial?

103
00:07:56,910 --> 00:07:59,249
Oh, his wife doesn't want him
to hear it.

104
00:07:59,250 --> 00:08:01,179
It's probably not juicy enough for her.

105
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:05,119
You surprise me there, Neil. Doesn't it
reach the highest levels of government?

106
00:08:05,120 --> 00:08:08,759
I doubt it. The evidence is clear-cut.
He tried to kill his lover.

107
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:10,690
Was Roxborough getting a job?

108
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:13,759
He was being offered something
in the Home Office.

109
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:16,525
He was told to clean up
his personal life first.

110
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:19,796
- Is that relevant?
- Might be if I were the prosecutor.

111
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:24,412
His sort prefer being on the outside
taking potshots at those on the inside.

112
00:08:25,611 --> 00:08:27,601
Perhaps it's good for his soul.

113
00:08:28,611 --> 00:08:31,350
Someone has to make the difficult
decisions for this country, John.

114
00:08:31,351 --> 00:08:32,841
Yes. I suppose so.

115
00:08:34,251 --> 00:08:37,369
Will you excuse me?
There's a judge I want to talk to.

116
00:08:41,190 --> 00:08:43,389
I'd like to talk some more
about your feelings

117
00:08:43,390 --> 00:08:45,560
at the time of your mother's death.

118
00:08:46,230 --> 00:08:48,359
I'm not sure there's that
much more to tell.

119
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:50,369
When she died, who did you turn to?

120
00:08:50,370 --> 00:08:52,390
- No one.
- Was your father around?

121
00:08:52,770 --> 00:08:53,770
Yes.

122
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:57,130
He was probably in a worse state
than I was.

123
00:08:58,610 --> 00:09:02,316
Did the 10-year-old know that
or did you work it out much later?

124
00:09:04,910 --> 00:09:08,310
My father was very angry.

125
00:09:10,690 --> 00:09:12,620
I think he blamed himself

126
00:09:15,220 --> 00:09:19,790
and he shut himself off emotionally
from my sister and me.

127
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:24,760
Who did you feel was to blame?

128
00:09:25,370 --> 00:09:28,194
No one. She was depressed.
She committed suicide.

129
00:09:30,540 --> 00:09:34,011
Is that what you understand now
or felt as that 10-year-old?

130
00:09:35,410 --> 00:09:38,528
What about that little boy
who's just lost his mother?

131
00:09:38,580 --> 00:09:40,160
What happened to him?

132
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:45,109
- Life goes on, doesn't it?
- Perhaps not always as it should.

133
00:09:49,290 --> 00:09:51,280
Were you happy from that point?

134
00:09:53,190 --> 00:09:55,120
Where did that little boy go?

135
00:10:05,940 --> 00:10:08,980
Um, I remember

136
00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:13,980
a cupboard under the stairs.

137
00:10:15,780 --> 00:10:19,016
I used to go there with Mum
during a thunderstorm, um...

138
00:10:19,420 --> 00:10:20,790
Hal Wow.

139
00:10:24,030 --> 00:10:25,610
I can still smell her.

140
00:10:28,230 --> 00:10:29,310
Vanilla.

141
00:10:33,200 --> 00:10:35,010
Well, she was a baker's wife.

142
00:10:37,110 --> 00:10:40,228
Did you often go back to that cupboard
after she died?

143
00:10:48,550 --> 00:10:49,660
(SNIFFS)

144
00:10:56,220 --> 00:10:57,300
(SIGHS)

145
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:10,230
I didn't ever want to come out.

146
00:11:31,430 --> 00:11:33,859
You know I have a history
with Monty Everard.

147
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:35,560
It is past history, Jo.

148
00:11:36,030 --> 00:11:38,089
When did the case go into his list?

149
00:11:38,430 --> 00:11:42,666
Was it before it was taken away from me
or after it was given back to me?

150
00:11:42,870 --> 00:11:46,440
Yes, I'm sorry about that mix-up.
You were our first choice.

151
00:11:47,610 --> 00:11:49,009
I hadn't realised you'd been approached

152
00:11:49,010 --> 00:11:51,379
when the brief was sent
to Newman Mason Allen.

153
00:11:51,380 --> 00:11:54,119
- Well, I know you'll be rigorous.
- If I'm convinced there's a case.

154
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:56,200
The DPP has taken a close interest.

155
00:11:56,550 --> 00:11:59,689
Because of who's involved,
he read the papers personally.

156
00:11:59,690 --> 00:12:03,514
- Did he see all the evidence?
- The case meets all the CPS tests.

157
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:07,937
We wouldn't want you going in to bat
unless you're absolutely confident.

158
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:09,950
What's worrying you?

159
00:12:20,270 --> 00:12:23,741
Would it offend if I brought
sausage and bacon to the table?

160
00:12:24,110 --> 00:12:25,979
Not half as much as it would the pig.

161
00:12:25,980 --> 00:12:27,120
(CHUCKLES)

162
00:12:27,350 --> 00:12:30,468
Well, one hardly relates it
to the once-living animal.

163
00:12:30,950 --> 00:12:32,689
Have you taken up the cause, Monty?

164
00:12:32,690 --> 00:12:35,889
Well, I might if I had a daughter
like yours pushing me.

165
00:12:35,890 --> 00:12:39,370
Ah. Not always the direction
I want to go in.

166
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:44,278
Well, I have a wife who's always
telling me how good protein is for me.

167
00:12:44,570 --> 00:12:46,270
Her family were farmers.

168
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:51,840
John, I know we haven't always
been cooperative.

169
00:12:52,870 --> 00:12:55,830
- I hope that can change.
- I'm sure it can, Monty.

170
00:13:01,350 --> 00:13:03,570
You have a murder case in your list.

171
00:13:04,750 --> 00:13:07,850
It involves the rather nasty rape
of a dead woman.

172
00:13:08,820 --> 00:13:12,409
If you don't particularly want it,
I wouldn't mind hearing it.

173
00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:14,408
It's pretty gruesome, Monty.

174
00:13:14,530 --> 00:13:16,499
Yeah, well, my wife wants
to stay down for it.

175
00:13:16,500 --> 00:13:19,250
She rather enjoys
the more gruesome cases.

176
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:22,599
Does this mean the Alan Roxborough case
is coming my way?

177
00:13:22,600 --> 00:13:25,012
- Well, would you mind swapping?
- Oh, no.

178
00:13:25,210 --> 00:13:27,709
Jo Mills won't be very pleased.
She's prosecuting.

179
00:13:27,710 --> 00:13:31,005
Well, John, you did go to rather
a lot of bother recently

180
00:13:31,880 --> 00:13:35,179
to prove that you don't have
a relationship with Mrs Mills.

181
00:13:35,180 --> 00:13:36,610
Yes, I did, didn't I?

182
00:13:36,920 --> 00:13:39,419
All right.
Ask your clerk to send the papers over.

183
00:13:39,420 --> 00:13:40,880
Do appreciate it.

184
00:13:43,320 --> 00:13:44,400
(DEED SHOUTS)

185
00:13:47,660 --> 00:13:48,950
(EXCLAIMING)

186
00:13:55,270 --> 00:13:56,470
(EXCLAIMS)

187
00:14:03,340 --> 00:14:06,590
- Is it true? You're hearing Roxborough?
- Who?

188
00:14:07,480 --> 00:14:09,790
Alan Roxborough, the MP.

189
00:14:11,150 --> 00:14:14,430
Oh, Alan Roxborough. Never heard of him.

190
00:14:14,960 --> 00:14:16,259
You didn't call me about it.

191
00:14:16,260 --> 00:14:18,589
Well, I thought you were concerned
to keep our relationship proper

192
00:14:18,590 --> 00:14:19,729
when you were in front of me.

193
00:14:19,730 --> 00:14:21,599
I am. I thought Legover was hearing it.

194
00:14:21,600 --> 00:14:24,210
Not any more.
Pre-trial review's on Thursday.

195
00:14:25,070 --> 00:14:27,600
Defence will try and knock it out,
but, hey,

196
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:31,840
why don't you stay and have a match?
We could have dinner afterwards.

197
00:14:32,870 --> 00:14:34,830
I have no appetite for either.

198
00:14:39,680 --> 00:14:41,269
The evidence is sound, Row.

199
00:14:41,350 --> 00:14:44,703
My job is to prosecute Roxborough
and secure a conviction.

200
00:14:45,190 --> 00:14:47,589
Unless you're telling me
there's a reason I shouldn't.

201
00:14:47,590 --> 00:14:49,589
No, no. I'm not saying
the police have done anything wrong.

202
00:14:49,590 --> 00:14:51,959
Just make sure
that you're across their evidence.

203
00:14:51,960 --> 00:14:53,959
You're a policeman, Row.
Why are you warning me?

204
00:14:53,960 --> 00:14:56,229
Well, you get a hammering,
the police do, too.

205
00:14:56,230 --> 00:14:57,840
Would you like some lunch?

206
00:14:58,170 --> 00:15:00,160
- Yes. That would be nice.
- Good.

207
00:15:02,470 --> 00:15:05,235
Uh... Oh, hang on a moment.
I won't be a second.

208
00:15:12,510 --> 00:15:15,805
- Are we on course?
- These things take time, Mr Daniels.

209
00:15:16,550 --> 00:15:18,830
We don't have time. Just get it done.

210
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:27,429
Without wishing to bring
undue pressure to bear,

211
00:15:27,430 --> 00:15:30,783
Whittakers are a major player
in weapons delivery systems.

212
00:15:30,870 --> 00:15:33,699
Roxborough's extravagant claims
about the unexplained deaths

213
00:15:33,700 --> 00:15:36,669
of some of their scientists are damaging
both them and the government.

214
00:15:36,670 --> 00:15:39,009
They could be held in camera
if there were real concerns.

215
00:15:39,010 --> 00:15:42,422
It was understood, Monty,
the trial would be in safe hands.

216
00:15:42,740 --> 00:15:47,035
- Indeed, I assured ministers it was.
- Why do you assume it no longer is?

217
00:15:47,050 --> 00:15:50,149
Nothing will persuade me of that
now that you've let the case go to Deed.

218
00:15:50,150 --> 00:15:51,689
It was agreed you'd hear it.

219
00:15:51,690 --> 00:15:54,419
Yes, but I took the view, Ian,
that we can't have Deed

220
00:15:54,420 --> 00:15:56,880
continually estranged from his brethren.

221
00:15:57,560 --> 00:16:00,559
While I share the sentiment, you could
have chosen a more appropriate case

222
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:03,699
with which to return him to the fold.
Can you get it back from him?

223
00:16:03,700 --> 00:16:05,599
You know the etiquette
as well as I do, Ian.

224
00:16:05,600 --> 00:16:07,210
Deed now has the case.

225
00:16:07,670 --> 00:16:11,009
Without over-stressing the situation,
that could be a complete disaster.

226
00:16:11,010 --> 00:16:13,410
Well, I'm sure you're exaggerating, Ian.

227
00:16:16,510 --> 00:16:20,510
- I'll see if I can't hook it back again.
- We would appreciate that.

228
00:16:24,890 --> 00:16:28,832
No, Monty. We shan't take the
Roxborough case back. It's too boring.

229
00:16:29,120 --> 00:16:31,310
It's politically expedient, my dear.

230
00:16:31,760 --> 00:16:34,290
Don't flannel me.
You're a High Court judge.

231
00:16:34,660 --> 00:16:36,699
You're supposed to be
above political pressure.

232
00:16:36,700 --> 00:16:40,860
Well, I know, but one does sometimes
have to meet them halfway.

233
00:16:41,670 --> 00:16:45,510
No. We'll stick with the murderer
who raped his victim.

234
00:16:49,780 --> 00:16:53,309
With your leave, My Lord, the Crown
would request a hearing in camera

235
00:16:53,310 --> 00:16:57,319
because of the sensitive nature of
some of the evidence likely to come out.

236
00:16:57,320 --> 00:16:59,489
I would need to hear
the most compelling argument.

237
00:16:59,490 --> 00:17:01,619
Mr Roxborough wasn't
a government minister, was he?

238
00:17:01,620 --> 00:17:03,589
- He didn't have access to state secrets.
- No.

239
00:17:03,590 --> 00:17:07,559
Well, he did put himself in possession
of a lot of secret information, My Lord.

240
00:17:07,560 --> 00:17:10,750
Mr Cantwell, without revealing
your defence, of course,

241
00:17:11,270 --> 00:17:12,918
will you be exposing secrets

242
00:17:13,170 --> 00:17:17,680
or making any revelations likely
to impede the function of government?

243
00:17:19,110 --> 00:17:22,570
That isn't the defence's intention,
My Lord, but who knows?

244
00:17:23,980 --> 00:17:25,470
I might get lucky.

245
00:17:28,850 --> 00:17:30,203
The fact is, Mrs Mills,

246
00:17:30,350 --> 00:17:33,949
the defendant has been accused
of a crime that he vigorously denies.

247
00:17:33,950 --> 00:17:36,959
To restrict reporting now would hardly
be fair on the defendant.

248
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:40,902
No, the court will weigh the relevance
of any evidence as it arises.

249
00:17:45,700 --> 00:17:47,810
Are we ready to empanel the jury?

250
00:17:50,540 --> 00:17:54,350
The man before you in the dock
is not a famous MP

251
00:17:55,280 --> 00:17:57,470
crusading on behalf of the underdog.

252
00:17:57,950 --> 00:18:02,009
Not a fearless fighter for justice
for the less privileged in society.

253
00:18:02,520 --> 00:18:04,690
He's a mere mortal like you and me.

254
00:18:05,390 --> 00:18:08,960
Full of the same frailties and fears
and anxieties.

255
00:18:09,560 --> 00:18:14,040
A man who, when pushed into a corner
and fearing exposure, lashed out,

256
00:18:15,460 --> 00:18:17,390
trying to silence the threat.

257
00:18:18,370 --> 00:18:21,800
He attempted to bash out
his former lover's brains

258
00:18:22,540 --> 00:18:25,835
with an iron frying pan
when he threatened to expose him.

259
00:18:26,410 --> 00:18:28,539
It wasn't the fact that they'd
been lovers that Donald Toombs

260
00:18:28,540 --> 00:18:30,150
threatened to expose. No.

261
00:18:30,480 --> 00:18:33,910
What Alan Roxborough so
desperately feared having revealed

262
00:18:34,720 --> 00:18:37,250
was his cynical and callous
private attitude

263
00:18:37,520 --> 00:18:39,980
to those he was publicly
at pains to help.

264
00:18:41,960 --> 00:18:46,313
The families of young scientists who
had died in unexplained circumstances.

265
00:18:46,330 --> 00:18:48,259
And to keep this truth
about himself secret,

266
00:18:48,260 --> 00:18:52,300
he was prepared to go to any lengths
to get back the letters

267
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:56,248
he had so recklessly written
to this young man

268
00:18:56,440 --> 00:18:59,920
in his own hand and on
House of Commons notepaper.

269
00:19:01,910 --> 00:19:03,660
He attempted to kill him.

270
00:19:04,040 --> 00:19:07,511
You'll read some of these letters
and decide for yourselves.

271
00:19:10,080 --> 00:19:12,949
Superintendent Craddock, when you
charged and cautioned the defendant,

272
00:19:12,950 --> 00:19:16,319
- did he say anything?
- Yes. He protested that he was an MP.

273
00:19:16,320 --> 00:19:18,729
He said the Home Secretary
would vouch for him.

274
00:19:18,730 --> 00:19:22,229
He also said that he was going
to the Home Office as a junior minister

275
00:19:22,230 --> 00:19:24,660
and that we'd be in trouble when he did.

276
00:19:25,170 --> 00:19:27,010
What was your response to this?

277
00:19:27,370 --> 00:19:30,782
I pointed out that we were
behaving correctly and politely.

278
00:19:36,380 --> 00:19:38,149
Were you intimidated by the threats?

279
00:19:38,150 --> 00:19:40,649
You hear all sorts of threats
from prisoners.

280
00:19:40,650 --> 00:19:43,979
This MP was going to the Home Office
where he'd have a direct influence

281
00:19:43,980 --> 00:19:47,216
Over yOur Career.
That didn't give you a moment's worry?

282
00:19:47,990 --> 00:19:50,167
It made me double-check the evidence.

283
00:19:50,390 --> 00:19:53,214
- You're pretty thorough, are you?
- I try to be.

284
00:19:53,930 --> 00:19:55,890
You aren't always so thorough.

285
00:19:57,630 --> 00:19:59,530
- Are you?
- I think I am, sir.

286
00:20:00,370 --> 00:20:03,680
Do you recall the case of Mohamed Aziz?

287
00:20:04,710 --> 00:20:08,769
A Muslim cleric you arrested six years
ago for inciting racial hatred?

288
00:20:10,140 --> 00:20:12,579
Well, you told him
that if he didn't shut up,

289
00:20:12,580 --> 00:20:15,816
you'd have the lads take him down
to a nice, quiet cell.

290
00:20:16,620 --> 00:20:20,503
- What did you mean by that?
- He was calling me a fat, racist pig.

291
00:20:20,990 --> 00:20:24,520
Was the nice, quiet cell
so that he could have a little rest?

292
00:20:25,390 --> 00:20:27,859
Or so that your boys
could beat the daylights out of him?

293
00:20:27,860 --> 00:20:30,625
That wasn't the finding
of the internal inquiry.

294
00:20:31,230 --> 00:20:33,999
How many white officers
make up an impartial inquiry?

295
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:35,869
What are you implying there,
Mr Cantwell?

296
00:20:35,870 --> 00:20:37,709
The hearing was biased or racist?

297
00:20:37,710 --> 00:20:42,063
My Lord, you know as well as anyone,
we're all victims of suspicion of bias

298
00:20:42,640 --> 00:20:44,810
when our colleagues investigate us.

299
00:20:49,580 --> 00:20:51,860
We have to assume that they were fair.

300
00:20:52,290 --> 00:20:54,879
The inquiry did exonerate me
and my officers.

301
00:20:55,360 --> 00:20:59,184
Oh, my point is not your supposed
racist attitude, Superintendent.

302
00:20:59,760 --> 00:21:02,600
Rather, erm, lack of care.

303
00:21:04,770 --> 00:21:06,880
Having been so abused by Mr Aziz,

304
00:21:07,500 --> 00:21:11,383
you then accepted the witness
statements against him at face value.

305
00:21:12,440 --> 00:21:15,264
You didn't bother to check
into their background.

306
00:21:15,880 --> 00:21:19,250
Both were card-carrying members
of the BNP

307
00:21:20,150 --> 00:21:22,610
with convictions for racist attack.

308
00:21:25,620 --> 00:21:28,870
As a result, the trial against Mr Aziz
was stopped.

309
00:21:32,030 --> 00:21:33,930
- Is that right?
- Yes.

310
00:21:34,760 --> 00:21:39,069
You then proceeded in the same fashion
against Mr Roxborough after he had...

311
00:21:39,070 --> 00:21:41,970
No. We checked and re-checked the facts.

312
00:21:43,870 --> 00:21:45,570
Indeed, Superintendent.

313
00:21:47,610 --> 00:21:48,660
Indeed.

314
00:21:49,640 --> 00:21:51,830
Mrs Mills, do you want to come back?

315
00:21:52,250 --> 00:21:53,950
Superintendent Craddock,

316
00:21:54,650 --> 00:21:57,819
were you able to establish who made
the attempt on the victim's life

317
00:21:57,820 --> 00:22:01,130
- on the 15th of March?
- Yes. It was the defendant.

318
00:22:02,360 --> 00:22:06,008
He'd gone to Toombs' flat
demanding back the remaining letters,

319
00:22:06,190 --> 00:22:09,014
which Toombs had threatened
to give to the press.

320
00:22:09,200 --> 00:22:10,699
Were the letters' contents of a nature

321
00:22:10,700 --> 00:22:13,399
likely to undermine his position
in parliament?

322
00:22:13,400 --> 00:22:15,812
My Lord, isn't that a matter
for the jury?

323
00:22:16,100 --> 00:22:18,806
- I rather think it is, Mrs Mills.
- I'm sorry.

324
00:22:20,410 --> 00:22:23,539
Superintendent, did you examine
the letters the defendant wrote

325
00:22:23,540 --> 00:22:25,910
- to Mr Toombs?
- I examined four letters.

326
00:22:25,911 --> 00:22:29,280
Four which Toombs had previously
returned to the defendant.

327
00:22:29,281 --> 00:22:33,458
- What was the tone of those letters?
- They said how much he loved him,

328
00:22:33,820 --> 00:22:36,719
how he was the only person
he felt he could relax with.

329
00:22:36,720 --> 00:22:39,389
In two of the letters,
he said he felt he was drowning

330
00:22:39,390 --> 00:22:41,820
and that he, Toombs,
was his life jacket.

331
00:22:43,160 --> 00:22:46,572
Referring to the families
of the dead scientists, he wrote,

332
00:22:47,500 --> 00:22:50,677
"These wretched Muslims
hang on to me like dead weights

333
00:22:51,240 --> 00:22:54,550
"with no concern that
they might be causing me to sink."

334
00:22:58,780 --> 00:23:03,079
- How long was your affair with Jo Mills?
- You mean when we slept together?

335
00:23:03,080 --> 00:23:05,420
If that's how you define a love affair.

336
00:23:05,820 --> 00:23:08,526
The love affair hasn't ended.
I still love her.

337
00:23:08,790 --> 00:23:12,459
Why do you feel compelled to have
physical relations with other women?

338
00:23:12,460 --> 00:23:13,959
I thought you were going to tell me.

339
00:23:13,960 --> 00:23:16,159
You know that's not what I do, Sir John.

340
00:23:16,160 --> 00:23:18,984
Wouldn't you be more comfortable
calling me John?

341
00:23:19,100 --> 00:23:22,571
- I'm quite comfortable, thank you.
- I don't think you are.

342
00:23:24,240 --> 00:23:26,050
Can you answer my question?

343
00:23:31,450 --> 00:23:33,410
It's almost entirely physical.

344
00:23:35,580 --> 00:23:38,522
Like the need to take a shower
morning and evening.

345
00:23:39,820 --> 00:23:42,526
What about for the women?
Does it mean nothing?

346
00:23:43,360 --> 00:23:45,470
I don't think it means much more.

347
00:23:45,930 --> 00:23:49,872
Wouldn't any of them want you
to pick up the phone, call them again?

348
00:23:50,360 --> 00:23:52,640
What, you mean having a relationship?

349
00:23:53,270 --> 00:23:55,800
My problem is
in letting relationships last.

350
00:23:56,070 --> 00:23:59,423
That's why I'm coming here.
I've been here five times now.

351
00:23:59,740 --> 00:24:01,339
I still want to pick up women.

352
00:24:01,340 --> 00:24:04,300
There's no magic formula, Sir John,
but we can try.

353
00:24:04,880 --> 00:24:08,351
Look, I want to have a long-term
relationship with Jo Mills.

354
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:11,619
She won't let me in because
she thinks I'm a serial womaniser.

355
00:24:11,620 --> 00:24:12,856
To prove that I'm not

356
00:24:12,920 --> 00:24:15,359
by having a long-term relationship
with somebody else,

357
00:24:15,360 --> 00:24:18,890
would preclude my having a relationship
with Jo, wouldn't it?

358
00:24:19,790 --> 00:24:24,299
Couldn't you equally prove this by not
having casual affairs with other women?

359
00:24:24,300 --> 00:24:27,948
- Then she'd have to take my word.
- You don't think she would?

360
00:24:28,500 --> 00:24:31,148
Would you take the word
of a serial womaniser?

361
00:24:32,670 --> 00:24:34,570
What is it you want from me?

362
00:24:35,480 --> 00:24:37,320
I want to make love to you.

363
00:24:39,680 --> 00:24:41,579
That often happens with male clients.

364
00:24:41,580 --> 00:24:44,228
Especially those who were adopted
as children.

365
00:24:45,290 --> 00:24:49,290
I think you want to make me one
of the women you have a problem with.

366
00:24:54,530 --> 00:24:56,720
I don't think you take me seriously.

367
00:24:57,700 --> 00:24:59,720
The trouble is, Sir John, I do.

368
00:25:00,500 --> 00:25:01,810
(INTERCOM BUZZING)

369
00:25:20,120 --> 00:25:22,885
- Hello.
- Paul, it's Jo. Have you got a moment?

370
00:25:22,960 --> 00:25:25,989
I'm going through the evidence of the
policeman who's up first thing tomorrow.

371
00:25:25,990 --> 00:25:27,696
- I'll come through.
- Thanks.

372
00:25:42,140 --> 00:25:43,258
Is there a problem?

373
00:25:43,340 --> 00:25:45,870
I hope not.
But according to the statements,

374
00:25:46,280 --> 00:25:49,104
Donald Toombs was attacked
at around ten to five.

375
00:25:49,220 --> 00:25:53,338
The MP was stopped in his car eight
miles away at one minute past five.

376
00:25:53,720 --> 00:25:55,759
He travelled the distance of eight miles

377
00:25:55,760 --> 00:25:58,459
between Donald Toombs' flat
and the stop point in 11 minutes.

378
00:25:58,460 --> 00:26:00,931
- Is that possible?
- If the police say so.

379
00:26:01,130 --> 00:26:03,999
Well, has anyone from our side
tried driving the route in 11 minutes?

380
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:05,869
Because it's certain the defence
will have done.

381
00:26:05,870 --> 00:26:08,599
It could have been 15. The witnesses
may not have been accurate.

382
00:26:08,600 --> 00:26:12,669
- Then it could have been less.
- Isn't this something for the defence?

383
00:26:12,670 --> 00:26:15,200
I want to make sure
they don't catch us out.

384
00:26:15,510 --> 00:26:18,000
I'll drive the route myself
in the morning.

385
00:26:20,150 --> 00:26:22,260
Do you want a drink before you go?

386
00:26:22,980 --> 00:26:25,730
Oh, I have a couple of briefs
in my pigeonhole.

387
00:26:26,190 --> 00:26:28,090
I must read them. Sorry.

388
00:26:44,610 --> 00:26:47,269
What was the defendant's attitude
when you stopped his car?

389
00:26:47,270 --> 00:26:49,440
Aggressive, miss. He threatened us.

390
00:26:50,140 --> 00:26:51,920
What was his opening remark?

391
00:26:52,280 --> 00:26:55,560
The specific threat
was the third thing he said.

392
00:26:56,120 --> 00:26:58,020
His attitude was aggressive.

393
00:26:58,420 --> 00:27:01,619
- Was he surprised you stopped him?
- Oh, he was outraged.

394
00:27:01,620 --> 00:27:05,359
When asked about the victim,
Donald Toombs, he denied knowing him.

395
00:27:05,360 --> 00:27:07,829
Did he give you a reason
for not knowing Mr Toombs?

396
00:27:07,830 --> 00:27:09,059
He said he'd forgotten him.

397
00:27:09,060 --> 00:27:12,220
Then when pressed,
he said he'd misheard the name.

398
00:27:12,770 --> 00:27:14,769
Can you describe
his physical appearance?

399
00:27:14,770 --> 00:27:17,239
Dishevelled.
His raincoat was smeared with blood.

400
00:27:17,240 --> 00:27:19,200
That's a lie. An absolute lie.

401
00:27:19,910 --> 00:27:22,263
I'm sure there will be a lot in evidence

402
00:27:22,340 --> 00:27:24,379
that you won't agree with,
Mr Roxborough.

403
00:27:24,380 --> 00:27:25,909
You will get your opportunity.

404
00:27:25,910 --> 00:27:27,779
I can't sit here
and listen to these lies.

405
00:27:27,780 --> 00:27:31,589
And we can't conduct a trial
with you refuting points from the dock.

406
00:27:31,590 --> 00:27:33,238
Your barrister will do that.

407
00:27:33,490 --> 00:27:35,830
- But these are blatant...
- Be quiet.

408
00:27:40,760 --> 00:27:41,900
Mrs Mills.

409
00:27:42,300 --> 00:27:44,653
How much blood was there on his clothes?

410
00:27:44,770 --> 00:27:48,299
It was four or five smudges.
Like he tried to wipe away a splash.

411
00:27:48,300 --> 00:27:49,889
No, no, no. This... This...

412
00:27:49,940 --> 00:27:53,909
Mr Roxborough, I know that you are used
to the cut and thrust of parliament,

413
00:27:53,910 --> 00:27:55,779
but when the Speaker
tells you to shut up,

414
00:27:55,780 --> 00:27:59,604
you shut up or find yourself removed.
Look upon me as the Speaker.

415
00:28:00,080 --> 00:28:02,219
With respect, My Lord,
I can't see howjustice can be done

416
00:28:02,220 --> 00:28:03,920
when I hear these lies.

417
00:28:06,490 --> 00:28:09,219
Do you feel yourself above the court,
Mr Roxborough?

418
00:28:09,220 --> 00:28:12,926
Because that is the impression
that this jury is likely to form.

419
00:28:14,130 --> 00:28:16,120
You must trust due process.

420
00:28:16,460 --> 00:28:19,637
You must trust your barrister.
You must trust the jury.

421
00:28:20,800 --> 00:28:24,624
They have given up their valuable time
to help to deliver justice.

422
00:28:27,270 --> 00:28:28,440
Mrs Mills.

423
00:28:29,180 --> 00:28:32,110
Can you look at exhibit nine, Constable?

424
00:28:42,590 --> 00:28:45,729
Is this the raincoat the defendant
was wearing when you stopped his car?

425
00:28:45,730 --> 00:28:47,720
- Yes, it is.
- No, that's a lie!

426
00:28:48,060 --> 00:28:50,359
- Dock officer, take him downstairs.
- You can't...

427
00:28:50,360 --> 00:28:52,599
My Lord, might I have a short
adjournment to speak to my client?

428
00:28:52,600 --> 00:28:54,270
He's clearly very upset.

429
00:28:54,600 --> 00:28:57,839
No, Mr Cantwell. You can speak to him
in the lunch adjournment.

430
00:28:57,840 --> 00:28:59,740
- Charming.
- Mrs Mills.

431
00:29:00,740 --> 00:29:03,639
Did you ask the defendant
about the blood, Constable?

432
00:29:03,640 --> 00:29:06,270
Yes. He said he didn't know
how it got there.

433
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:10,104
I then asked him if it was his blood
and he said,

434
00:29:11,950 --> 00:29:15,140
"Yes. It must be.
I had a nosebleed in the car."

435
00:29:15,660 --> 00:29:17,300
- Was it his blood?
- No.

436
00:29:18,160 --> 00:29:20,470
It was from the victim, Donald Toombs.

437
00:29:22,100 --> 00:29:23,759
That policeman is lying
through his teeth.

438
00:29:23,760 --> 00:29:25,469
I didn't have a nosebleed.
It's all lies.

439
00:29:25,470 --> 00:29:28,250
Yes, I'm sure you're right.

440
00:29:30,540 --> 00:29:33,230
- The police are lying, Brian.
- Of course.

441
00:29:37,140 --> 00:29:40,480
Our time will come.
Now, let me talk to the judge,

442
00:29:41,050 --> 00:29:43,079
see if I can persuade him
to let you back up.

443
00:29:43,080 --> 00:29:47,089
If I can, you have to promise me
not to make another sound in the dock.

444
00:29:47,090 --> 00:29:48,490
Promise me that.

445
00:29:52,360 --> 00:29:53,360
Good.

446
00:29:56,860 --> 00:29:58,590
Do you have this sent in?

447
00:30:02,140 --> 00:30:06,800
Mrs Mills. My name's Michael Rudkin.
I'm a solicitor. Hi. Erm...

448
00:30:08,440 --> 00:30:10,379
I have some information about this case.

449
00:30:10,380 --> 00:30:12,379
What sort of information, Mr Rudkin?

450
00:30:12,380 --> 00:30:14,810
Well, I thought someone
would contact me.

451
00:30:15,420 --> 00:30:18,068
I witnessed the police
stopping Mr Roxborough.

452
00:30:18,490 --> 00:30:20,540
He didn't have blood on his coat.

453
00:30:23,120 --> 00:30:24,829
It will be very damaging to our case

454
00:30:24,830 --> 00:30:27,029
if you reveal this evidence
from Michael Rudkin, Jo.

455
00:30:27,030 --> 00:30:28,780
We don't have any option.

456
00:30:29,560 --> 00:30:32,829
Unless you subscribe to the belief
in winning at any cost.

457
00:30:32,830 --> 00:30:35,242
Jo, we are looking
to secure a conviction.

458
00:30:35,500 --> 00:30:37,610
Not if he proves not guilty.

459
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:39,670
Are you ready, Mrs Mills?

460
00:30:40,010 --> 00:30:42,410
Could I have just one moment,
Mrs Cooper?

461
00:30:49,150 --> 00:30:50,960
Brian, could I have a word?

462
00:30:51,990 --> 00:30:54,689
Do you remember seeing a witness
in the vicinity

463
00:30:54,690 --> 00:30:56,560
when the police stopped you?

464
00:30:57,660 --> 00:30:59,543
Was there one? What's he saying?

465
00:30:59,790 --> 00:31:03,499
Certainly nothing that's going
to help the police with their case.

466
00:31:03,500 --> 00:31:06,630
Oh. I suppose there was someone nearby.

467
00:31:08,740 --> 00:31:11,730
I must have forgotten about it. Yes.

468
00:31:14,810 --> 00:31:17,330
Constable Welkin,
you have a good memory?

469
00:31:18,710 --> 00:31:21,358
- I think so, sir.
- Has it ever let you down?

470
00:31:22,320 --> 00:31:24,100
It's pretty accurate, sir.

471
00:31:25,090 --> 00:31:29,219
Yet you used your notebook throughout
your evidence in chief. Why was that?

472
00:31:29,220 --> 00:31:31,240
The Judge gave me permission, sir.

473
00:31:31,590 --> 00:31:34,429
Why did you find it necessary
to ask for that permission?

474
00:31:34,430 --> 00:31:37,783
It means I'm absolutely accurate
in what I tell the court.

475
00:31:38,430 --> 00:31:40,799
I'm sure we're all
most grateful, Constable.

476
00:31:40,800 --> 00:31:43,840
- Is it a contemporaneous note?
- Yes, sir. It is.

477
00:31:44,340 --> 00:31:46,409
You took it in the street
as you interviewed Mr Roxborough.

478
00:31:46,410 --> 00:31:48,479
No, sir. When I got back to the station.

479
00:31:48,480 --> 00:31:52,050
Ah. Contemporaneously recorded
after the event.

480
00:31:52,650 --> 00:31:55,430
Mr Cantwell, experience over the years

481
00:31:55,920 --> 00:32:00,215
has suggested that notes made when
the police officer is able to make them

482
00:32:00,220 --> 00:32:02,103
are accepted as contemporaneous.

483
00:32:02,420 --> 00:32:04,970
Yes. A lawyer's contemporaneous,

484
00:32:05,860 --> 00:32:09,520
- everyone else's sometime later.
- Yes. All right. I see.

485
00:32:10,100 --> 00:32:13,140
It's not always possible or reasonable

486
00:32:13,630 --> 00:32:16,439
for an officer to make a note
when the incident occurs.

487
00:32:16,440 --> 00:32:18,440
It's no reflection on the officer,

488
00:32:18,740 --> 00:32:22,093
though, nevertheless,
Mr Cantwell does have a valid point.

489
00:32:22,540 --> 00:32:23,620
My Lord.

490
00:32:24,740 --> 00:32:26,909
How long after the stop
did you make the note?

491
00:32:26,910 --> 00:32:28,580
Fifteen minutes at the most.

492
00:32:29,150 --> 00:32:32,209
- You made the stop at one minute past 5.
- Yes, sir.

493
00:32:33,020 --> 00:32:34,789
You took Mr Roxborough to the station,

494
00:32:34,790 --> 00:32:37,496
found the duty inspector,
explained the arrest.

495
00:32:37,820 --> 00:32:40,190
You got Mr Roxborough a cup of tea,

496
00:32:40,590 --> 00:32:43,199
took his fingerprints,
let him make a phone call,

497
00:32:43,200 --> 00:32:47,069
took his car keys, arranged for his car
to be brought to the station,

498
00:32:47,070 --> 00:32:50,260
and wrote your notes
all within 15 minutes?

499
00:32:50,800 --> 00:32:52,940
- Yes.
- At least an hour, wasn't it?

500
00:32:53,510 --> 00:32:56,670
- No. Fifteen minutes.
- Fifteen minutes!

501
00:32:58,380 --> 00:33:00,660
- Come off it!- Mr Cantwell.

502
00:33:02,680 --> 00:33:04,610
These aren't notes, are they?

503
00:33:05,920 --> 00:33:07,350
This is a script.

504
00:33:08,390 --> 00:33:10,880
PC Welkin's little drama.

505
00:33:12,760 --> 00:33:16,702
Time is vitally important in this case,
Constable Welkin, is it not?

506
00:33:17,030 --> 00:33:19,442
It's always important
to be accurate, sir.

507
00:33:19,570 --> 00:33:23,110
I repeat.
Time is vitally important here.

508
00:33:23,840 --> 00:33:25,770
The times are accurate, sir.

509
00:33:26,640 --> 00:33:29,346
The crime supposedly committed
by Mr Roxborough

510
00:33:29,540 --> 00:33:32,440
took place at 16:50,
11 minutes before the stop.

511
00:33:33,180 --> 00:33:36,004
Do you believe it's possible
to drive eight miles

512
00:33:36,020 --> 00:33:39,420
- in traffic in 11 minutes?
- That's what he did, sir.

513
00:33:40,350 --> 00:33:45,450
Well, that would mean he's travelling
at 43.6 miles per hour all the way.

514
00:33:47,630 --> 00:33:49,210
Is that possible?

515
00:33:49,500 --> 00:33:52,206
For a man fleeing from a crime,
I'd say it was.

516
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:58,070
Constable Welkin,
have you always been a constable?

517
00:33:59,510 --> 00:34:00,650
Yes, sir.

518
00:34:01,810 --> 00:34:04,210
Even when you were in
the Special Branch?

519
00:34:07,280 --> 00:34:10,986
- You were in the Special Branch?
- I was seconded to them, sir.

520
00:34:11,350 --> 00:34:12,990
- Did you know?
- No.

521
00:34:13,320 --> 00:34:15,070
For two and a half years.

522
00:34:16,220 --> 00:34:20,456
You went back into uniform one week
before the arrest of Alan Roxborough.

523
00:34:22,230 --> 00:34:26,099
Were you previously part of the squad
conducting surveillance on the MP?

524
00:34:26,100 --> 00:34:27,390
No, I wasn't.

525
00:34:28,370 --> 00:34:31,069
There was surveillance on this MP,
wasn't there?

526
00:34:31,070 --> 00:34:33,000
I don't know about that, sir.

527
00:34:34,110 --> 00:34:37,758
This whole story about getting
the call and finding the suspect

528
00:34:38,140 --> 00:34:40,570
with blood on his coat is a fabrication.

529
00:34:42,620 --> 00:34:45,989
An elaborate conspiracy to convict
Alan Roxborough, is it not?

530
00:34:45,990 --> 00:34:48,330
- No, sir.
- I wonder.

531
00:34:50,720 --> 00:34:54,250
Why did you transfer out of
Special Branch, Constable Welkin?

532
00:34:55,130 --> 00:34:57,300
- My time was up, sir.
- Was it?

533
00:34:59,100 --> 00:35:01,865
- You mean the secondment had ended.
- Yes, sir.

534
00:35:02,100 --> 00:35:05,159
You're sure you were seconded
to the Branch, are you?

535
00:35:05,410 --> 00:35:07,580
- Yes, sir.
- That's interesting.

536
00:35:08,840 --> 00:35:12,488
Because on your police record
it says you applied. Why is that?

537
00:35:15,080 --> 00:35:17,220
- That's a mistake, sir.
- A mistake?

538
00:35:19,750 --> 00:35:22,927
Was your reason for leaving the Branch
another mistake?

539
00:35:23,860 --> 00:35:25,970
My secondment had come to an end.

540
00:35:27,160 --> 00:35:30,380
Your record says you have
great aptitude for the work.

541
00:35:31,900 --> 00:35:34,842
Not someone they usually
let go of lightly, surely.

542
00:35:35,500 --> 00:35:36,990
I don't know, sir.

543
00:35:38,710 --> 00:35:40,760
Well, we'll let the jury decide.

544
00:35:47,680 --> 00:35:49,960
Mrs Mills, do you want to re-examine?

545
00:35:53,150 --> 00:35:55,170
Constable, is it possible to drive

546
00:35:55,560 --> 00:35:58,219
from Donald Toombs' flat to where
you stopped the defendant,

547
00:35:58,220 --> 00:36:00,590
a distance of eight miles,
in 11 minutes?

548
00:36:01,230 --> 00:36:02,660
Yes, it is, miss.

549
00:36:02,960 --> 00:36:04,699
Would he have needed
to break the speed limit?

550
00:36:04,700 --> 00:36:06,450
Yes, miss. Several times.

551
00:36:07,400 --> 00:36:10,539
But someone fleeing from a crime
wouldn't consider that.

552
00:36:10,540 --> 00:36:13,717
Coop, would you ask Dr Crawcheck
to join me in chambers

553
00:36:14,040 --> 00:36:17,511
- at the adjournment for tea?
- JO: ...and how long it took.

554
00:36:18,110 --> 00:36:20,330
- Dr Crawcheck.
- Thanks, Coop.

555
00:36:20,710 --> 00:36:25,005
- Shall I bring your tea now, Judge?
- Thank you, that would be very nice.

556
00:36:27,890 --> 00:36:31,538
- I shouldn't meet you like this.
- 'Cause of those boundaries?

557
00:36:32,530 --> 00:36:36,178
Psychotherapists are often
no more consistent than anyone else.

558
00:36:38,000 --> 00:36:41,099
I'm not allowed to sit down
in your presence. Charlie said.

559
00:36:41,100 --> 00:36:43,530
Well, you can with my permission.
Please.

560
00:36:49,710 --> 00:36:52,122
Charlie said you'd been
recently divorced.

561
00:36:52,310 --> 00:36:54,309
I don't talk about myself with clients.

562
00:36:54,310 --> 00:36:58,605
Yes. It's odd that, isn't it? Because
we talk to Charlie about each other.

563
00:36:59,150 --> 00:37:02,170
Yes. Another one of
those inconsistencies.

564
00:37:05,330 --> 00:37:08,930
So did you come here to observe me?
Or Mrs Mills?

565
00:37:10,200 --> 00:37:12,612
I must confess to being curious
about her.

566
00:37:12,870 --> 00:37:16,694
- Isn't that breaking the rules?
- I do break the rules sometimes.

567
00:37:25,350 --> 00:37:27,109
- Hello?
- Paul, did I wake you?

568
00:37:27,110 --> 00:37:28,310
Fat chance.

569
00:37:28,680 --> 00:37:31,079
I was watching News 24.
We've got three minutes.

570
00:37:31,080 --> 00:37:33,963
Good. I want you to get on
to the CPS first thing.

571
00:37:34,350 --> 00:37:37,419
We don't have the transcripts
of the calls to the police area car

572
00:37:37,420 --> 00:37:39,729
that Constables Welkin and Bengy
were in.

573
00:37:39,730 --> 00:37:41,959
I'm surprised the defenc e
hasn't asked for them.

574
00:37:41,960 --> 00:37:43,040
CPS'll have a fit!

575
00:37:43,300 --> 00:37:45,418
Well, it should have been disclosed.

576
00:37:46,230 --> 00:37:48,160
You might get a big surprise.

577
00:37:48,500 --> 00:37:51,139
Why didn't you go to the defence
with your concerns, Row?

578
00:37:51,140 --> 00:37:53,860
Well, as a policeman,
I can only go so far, Jo.

579
00:37:54,940 --> 00:37:58,235
You mightn't be a policeman much longer
if they find out.

580
00:37:58,780 --> 00:38:00,830
I'll have to retire soon anyway.

581
00:38:02,580 --> 00:38:05,389
Give me five minutes to finish this
and call home to see if Mark's okay,

582
00:38:05,390 --> 00:38:07,920
- then we'll eat.
- I'll visit the plumbing.

583
00:38:10,360 --> 00:38:11,470
- Row?
- Mmm?

584
00:38:12,760 --> 00:38:15,029
Why not say what it is
you hope I'll find?

585
00:38:15,030 --> 00:38:16,610
You're a bright girl.

586
00:38:27,210 --> 00:38:28,520
(RINGING)

587
00:38:34,250 --> 00:38:36,721
- Row Colemore's phone.
- MAN: Where is he?

588
00:38:36,980 --> 00:38:38,749
Well, he stepped out. I can get him.

589
00:38:38,750 --> 00:38:41,456
- Tell him to call Frank Daniels.
- Is there...

590
00:38:43,660 --> 00:38:47,249
- Who's Frank Daniels?
- Oh, he's a prospective employer. Why?

591
00:38:47,830 --> 00:38:49,301
He wants you to call him.

592
00:38:49,500 --> 00:38:51,912
- He's very abrupt.
- He's downright rude.

593
00:38:53,030 --> 00:38:55,639
Look, Jo, I'd better do it now.
Would you excuse me?

594
00:38:55,640 --> 00:38:56,690
Course.

595
00:39:04,580 --> 00:39:06,345
Mr Daniels, it's Row Colemore.

596
00:39:06,910 --> 00:39:09,440
I found someone
who can fix the police tape.

597
00:39:09,520 --> 00:39:11,119
The CPS won't know what's going on.

598
00:39:11,120 --> 00:39:13,826
Good. Whittakers deserves
to go down the tubes.

599
00:39:14,690 --> 00:39:18,220
Roxborough was making sure of that.
No, I want him acquitted.

600
00:39:20,130 --> 00:39:23,610
My taking over the company
will be good for the country.

601
00:39:26,630 --> 00:39:28,739
We have another problem, Mr Daniels.

602
00:39:28,740 --> 00:39:31,623
The solicitor, Michael Rudkin,
is getting nervous.

603
00:39:32,270 --> 00:39:35,310
No. You've got the problem, Row.
Now deal with it.

604
00:39:46,890 --> 00:39:48,620
(INAUDIBLE)

605
00:39:57,160 --> 00:40:00,631
What is it you're after, Jo?
The prosecution case is strong.

606
00:40:00,870 --> 00:40:02,539
I don't want it falling apart.

607
00:40:02,540 --> 00:40:06,188
How? We've still got Donald
Toombs' evidence. It's very strong.

608
00:40:06,340 --> 00:40:08,069
Are you ready to start, Mrs Mills?

609
00:40:08,070 --> 00:40:11,188
- Just give us a moment.
- Well, the judge is waiting.

610
00:40:13,880 --> 00:40:18,057
- Elliott, what nonsense is this?
- The log transcripts tell us nothing.

611
00:40:18,720 --> 00:40:22,180
The fact is, we don't have them.
They're lost.

612
00:40:23,220 --> 00:40:25,830
Well, fine, then I'll have
the area car tape.

613
00:40:30,300 --> 00:40:32,569
Do we know what sort of trouble
Mrs Mills is having?

614
00:40:32,570 --> 00:40:36,218
- No. She didn't say, Judge.
- Let's go and find out, shall we?

615
00:40:38,840 --> 00:40:40,450
(GAVEL POUNDING)

616
00:40:40,740 --> 00:40:42,170
USHER: All rise.

617
00:40:49,350 --> 00:40:51,549
Mrs Mills,
you're calling your next witness?

618
00:40:51,550 --> 00:40:53,860
With your leave, we call Carlos Fedor.

619
00:40:56,320 --> 00:41:00,850
Mr Fedor, can you tell the court what
your relationship is with the defendant?

620
00:41:01,890 --> 00:41:05,400
Mr Alan said he'd get me papers
to live here always.

621
00:41:06,570 --> 00:41:08,512
Where did you meet the defendant?

622
00:41:08,630 --> 00:41:11,880
Yes. We met at the Blue Flame club.

623
00:41:13,610 --> 00:41:15,963
He takes me around the House of Commons.

624
00:41:15,980 --> 00:41:18,275
And what is your relationship with him?

625
00:41:20,580 --> 00:41:22,540
He made love to me many times.

626
00:41:22,980 --> 00:41:24,410
(ALL SNICKERING)

627
00:41:27,390 --> 00:41:28,685
Did he give you money?

628
00:41:28,720 --> 00:41:31,210
- My Lord...
- No. Good try, though.

629
00:41:33,590 --> 00:41:35,550
Answer the question, Mr Fedor.

630
00:41:36,160 --> 00:41:39,030
Yes. Many times. Much money.

631
00:41:40,200 --> 00:41:42,869
Do you still have a relationship
with the defendant?

632
00:41:42,870 --> 00:41:45,860
No. He made the police
to send me back to Rio.

633
00:41:47,240 --> 00:41:50,476
How old were you when Mr Roxborough
met you in the club?

634
00:41:51,780 --> 00:41:55,030
- 17.
- 17 going on 37.

635
00:41:57,020 --> 00:41:59,844
- I don't understand.
- I think you do, Mr Fedor.

636
00:42:00,190 --> 00:42:02,955
Weren't you a regular visitor
to the Blue Flame,

637
00:42:03,020 --> 00:42:06,962
a nightclub frequented by homosexual
prostitutes of whom you're one?

638
00:42:08,360 --> 00:42:10,380
You do sell your body, Mr Fedor?

639
00:42:10,760 --> 00:42:14,369
And had been doing so for a good while
when Mr Roxborough met you.

640
00:42:14,370 --> 00:42:16,869
He got the police
to throw me out of the country.

641
00:42:16,870 --> 00:42:18,270
For shoplifting.

642
00:42:20,040 --> 00:42:24,140
The fact is, Mr Fedor,
you are an habitual shoplifter

643
00:42:25,310 --> 00:42:27,770
with a string of convictions,
are you not?

644
00:42:28,580 --> 00:42:31,580
He say he help me,
but he tell the police I no good.

645
00:42:31,620 --> 00:42:34,915
A fact I'm sure the jury will find
inescapable, Mr Fedor.

646
00:42:35,250 --> 00:42:37,090
I am sitting down, My Lord.

647
00:42:40,560 --> 00:42:43,560
JO: What time did Mr Roxborough
arrive at your flat?

648
00:42:44,100 --> 00:42:48,790
The first time about 4:30.
He phoned to ask if he could come over.

649
00:42:49,500 --> 00:42:52,939
It wasn't me he wanted to see. He just
wanted the letters he'd written me.

650
00:42:52,940 --> 00:42:55,839
What was in the letters
that made a visit so necessary?

651
00:42:55,840 --> 00:42:57,890
He'd been indiscreet in them.

652
00:42:58,310 --> 00:43:00,781
As an MP,
he'd made his reputation fighting

653
00:43:01,180 --> 00:43:04,949
his lost causes, as he called them.
He made a lot of noise in parliament

654
00:43:04,950 --> 00:43:08,774
about the unexplained deaths
at Whittakers, the armaments company.

655
00:43:10,160 --> 00:43:12,866
He said he was worrying
people high up so much,

656
00:43:13,530 --> 00:43:15,429
he was being offered a job
in the government.

657
00:43:15,430 --> 00:43:18,842
- Did he tell you what sort of a job?
- In the Home Office.

658
00:43:19,700 --> 00:43:23,406
But the families of those who died
were pushing him for answers.

659
00:43:24,600 --> 00:43:27,970
He wrote to me saying
how these grasping people

660
00:43:28,510 --> 00:43:32,158
were draining him, and nothing
he ever did was enough for them.

661
00:43:33,150 --> 00:43:36,150
He wished they'd all commit suicide
like their sons.

662
00:43:39,190 --> 00:43:43,190
- Whose property were these letters?
- Mine. He'd written them to me.

663
00:43:43,360 --> 00:43:45,890
- Did he ask for them back?
- Several times.

664
00:43:46,530 --> 00:43:50,759
- I told him he couldn't have them.
- Did he threaten you over the letters?

665
00:43:50,760 --> 00:43:53,950
He said I'd be sorry
if I didn't give them up.

666
00:43:54,670 --> 00:43:55,870
Did he say why?

667
00:43:56,100 --> 00:43:59,170
He said he'd make sure the police got me

668
00:44:01,040 --> 00:44:03,030
when he got to the Home Office.

669
00:44:03,640 --> 00:44:06,040
I laughed.
I said he'd never get the job.

670
00:44:06,780 --> 00:44:08,800
And that's when he went for me.

671
00:44:09,780 --> 00:44:11,710
Did he press home his attack?

672
00:44:13,420 --> 00:44:16,362
He held me down
and demanded the remaining letters.

673
00:44:17,190 --> 00:44:19,029
How many letters in all
did he write to you?

674
00:44:19,030 --> 00:44:23,540
Seven. Like a fool, I'd given four back
when he asked for them.

675
00:44:24,860 --> 00:44:27,331
- Did you give him the other letters?
- No.

676
00:44:28,200 --> 00:44:32,377
What did he do when you refused to
give him the three remaining letters?

677
00:44:32,410 --> 00:44:34,340
He tried to have sex with me.

678
00:44:34,870 --> 00:44:36,800
And when I wouldn't, he left.

679
00:44:37,180 --> 00:44:39,533
- Was that the last you saw of him?
- No.

680
00:44:41,710 --> 00:44:45,319
He came back about 10 minutes later
and let himself in with a key.

681
00:44:45,320 --> 00:44:47,280
I didn't even know he had a key.

682
00:44:47,620 --> 00:44:51,150
I was in the kitchen.
The radio was on, so I didn't hear him.

683
00:44:52,730 --> 00:44:55,966
He came up behind me
and put his hands around my throat.

684
00:44:56,001 --> 00:44:57,991
I thought he was gonna kill me.

685
00:44:59,771 --> 00:45:02,141
I mean, my eyes were bulging.

686
00:45:03,001 --> 00:45:05,649
My ears were bursting.
I could hardly breathe.

687
00:45:07,211 --> 00:45:10,094
He said, "I want those letters,
you little queer,"

688
00:45:11,510 --> 00:45:13,290
and threw me to the floor.

689
00:45:15,150 --> 00:45:16,760
The next thing I knew,

690
00:45:17,080 --> 00:45:21,210
I had a tremendous pain in my kidneys
where he'd knee-dropped me.

691
00:45:24,220 --> 00:45:27,290
He kept saying, "Give me the letters,"

692
00:45:29,860 --> 00:45:32,110
and started slapping me around my head

693
00:45:32,500 --> 00:45:35,780
and grinding my face
into the floor. Sorry.

694
00:45:40,470 --> 00:45:42,490
Grinding my face into the floor.

695
00:45:46,180 --> 00:45:47,230
Then...

696
00:45:49,320 --> 00:45:51,780
Then it felt as if
my head had split open.

697
00:45:54,220 --> 00:45:56,500
He'd hit me with the iron frying pan.

698
00:46:00,230 --> 00:46:02,570
I just started screaming and screaming.

699
00:46:05,830 --> 00:46:08,900
Then suddenly, the blows stopped

700
00:46:11,500 --> 00:46:13,990
and I tried to crawl to the door

701
00:46:16,740 --> 00:46:19,050
and when I got there,
my neighbour Linda

702
00:46:20,350 --> 00:46:22,840
had come in to find out
if I was all right.

703
00:46:26,550 --> 00:46:28,690
- I'm sorry.
- Thank you, Mr Toombs.

704
00:46:36,100 --> 00:46:38,470
Do you need to take a break, Mr Toombs?

705
00:46:39,800 --> 00:46:41,670
I'd rather get it over with.

706
00:46:42,800 --> 00:46:44,030
Mr Cantwell.

707
00:46:44,500 --> 00:46:45,670
Mr Toombs,

708
00:46:47,110 --> 00:46:50,346
let us suppose for a moment
that it wasn't Mr Roxborough

709
00:46:50,710 --> 00:46:53,679
who came back to your flat
that Sunday afternoon, but someone else.

710
00:46:53,680 --> 00:46:56,563
- It was him.
- But supposing it was someone else.

711
00:46:57,950 --> 00:47:00,833
You didn't see him.
He approached you from behind.

712
00:47:03,060 --> 00:47:04,960
Who else could it have been?

713
00:47:07,530 --> 00:47:10,648
- I don't know. I didn't see.
- Quite. You didn't see.

714
00:47:12,430 --> 00:47:15,300
- Who has a key to your flat?
- No one. Just me.

715
00:47:15,940 --> 00:47:19,639
- Have you lost a key or lent one out?
- No. I've got the only keys.

716
00:47:19,640 --> 00:47:21,660
You claim Mr Roxborough has one.

717
00:47:22,640 --> 00:47:25,100
Yet, in his effects
at the police station,

718
00:47:26,050 --> 00:47:28,040
no key to your flat was found.

719
00:47:28,880 --> 00:47:30,140
How was that?

720
00:47:31,150 --> 00:47:33,915
- He must have thrown it away.
- Thrown it away?

721
00:47:34,550 --> 00:47:36,300
Having no further need of it?

722
00:47:36,620 --> 00:47:40,385
Having got the three most dangerous,
threatening letters off you?

723
00:47:41,590 --> 00:47:42,730
- Yes?
- Yes.

724
00:47:43,460 --> 00:47:45,559
Did you tell Mr Roxborough
where you had hidden the letters?

725
00:47:45,560 --> 00:47:47,669
No, of course I didn't.
I'm not stupid, you know.

726
00:47:47,670 --> 00:47:51,480
Yet this second mysterious
visitor to your flat

727
00:47:52,200 --> 00:47:55,330
- found them and took them.
- It was Alan!

728
00:47:56,280 --> 00:47:58,240
Then where did the letters go?

729
00:47:59,250 --> 00:48:02,079
They weren't in Mr Roxborough's
possession at the police station,

730
00:48:02,080 --> 00:48:03,130
nor in his car.

731
00:48:03,350 --> 00:48:07,089
Or do you imagine he threw them
from the window for anyone to read?

732
00:48:07,090 --> 00:48:09,490
Well, I don't know. Perhaps he ate them.

733
00:48:15,030 --> 00:48:18,089
You do see what I'm getting at,
don't you, Mr Toombs?

734
00:48:19,500 --> 00:48:22,559
There is a real possibility
that it was someone other

735
00:48:22,600 --> 00:48:24,699
than Mr Roxborough
who came back to your flat.

736
00:48:24,700 --> 00:48:28,171
Now, on November the 9th,
you made a complaint to the police

737
00:48:29,070 --> 00:48:31,379
saying you thought you were
being followed

738
00:48:31,380 --> 00:48:34,145
and that you thought your flat
was being bugged.

739
00:48:35,150 --> 00:48:36,974
Is there any substance to this?

740
00:48:37,180 --> 00:48:39,549
The police said there wasn't,
but then, they would, wouldn't they?

741
00:48:39,550 --> 00:48:42,845
- To protect Alan.
- Why do you think this was happening?

742
00:48:43,120 --> 00:48:46,003
Because I was seeing an MP
who was making trouble.

743
00:48:46,860 --> 00:48:48,989
I mean, I was glad when he broke it off.

744
00:48:48,990 --> 00:48:51,199
I was sick of all that stuff.
It was worrying me.

745
00:48:51,200 --> 00:48:53,830
Then the possibility that someone was

746
00:48:55,330 --> 00:48:59,340
insinuating himself into your flat
comes all the more alive.

747
00:49:00,840 --> 00:49:02,950
Now, when the relationship ended,

748
00:49:03,640 --> 00:49:06,549
did you write to Mr Roxborough
demanding money from him?

749
00:49:06,550 --> 00:49:07,550
No.

750
00:49:08,680 --> 00:49:10,170
I asked for a loan.

751
00:49:11,220 --> 00:49:16,700
I had psoriasis, I was out of work
and I was behind with my rent.

752
00:49:18,060 --> 00:49:19,730
Well, which is it?

753
00:49:21,430 --> 00:49:25,489
Because you were out of work,
couldn't pay the rent or had itchy skin?

754
00:49:29,070 --> 00:49:30,090
(CLEARING THROAT)

755
00:49:30,300 --> 00:49:32,410
"If you don't let me have ￡1,000,

756
00:49:33,240 --> 00:49:35,535
"I'll send your letters
to the DailyMail

757
00:49:35,670 --> 00:49:38,435
"and tell them
the whole dirty truth about you."

758
00:49:39,480 --> 00:49:42,716
Did you write that to Mr Roxborough
on the 1st of March?

759
00:49:45,150 --> 00:49:46,200
I can't remember.

760
00:49:46,420 --> 00:49:49,049
You can't remember?
Do you often write letters like this?

761
00:49:49,050 --> 00:49:52,019
- Are you exhibiting this, Mr Cantwell?
- I will be, My Lord.

762
00:49:52,020 --> 00:49:55,829
- Do you have a handwriting expert?
- Oh, you bet we do. Well, Mr Toombs?

763
00:49:55,830 --> 00:49:57,536
Mr Toombs, before you answer,

764
00:49:57,800 --> 00:50:00,629
has anyone advised you that
you might expose yourself

765
00:50:00,630 --> 00:50:02,630
to the serious charge of blackmail

766
00:50:03,000 --> 00:50:05,870
under Section 21 of the 1968 Theft Act?

767
00:50:06,840 --> 00:50:09,439
Does Your Lordship
have to hand him the lifeline?

768
00:50:09,440 --> 00:50:12,852
Come off it, Mr Cantwell.
It's the oldest rule in the book.

769
00:50:13,710 --> 00:50:17,020
Mr Toombs, no one is
bound to answer any question

770
00:50:17,550 --> 00:50:21,374
if the answer might lay them open
to a criminal charge. Thank you.

771
00:50:22,320 --> 00:50:24,438
Let me put it delicately, Mr Toombs.

772
00:50:24,760 --> 00:50:27,830
The truth is,
you are not a victim of crime.

773
00:50:30,660 --> 00:50:32,740
- You are the face of crime.
- What?

774
00:50:33,100 --> 00:50:36,640
A scringing, homosexual blackmailer!

775
00:50:39,840 --> 00:50:43,429
JO: How many times have
you met with the defendant, Mr Toombs?

776
00:50:43,510 --> 00:50:45,680
I don't know. Loads of times.

777
00:50:46,080 --> 00:50:49,109
Would you describe your relationship
with him as intimate?

778
00:50:49,110 --> 00:50:51,480
Yes. It was for almost a year.

779
00:50:52,550 --> 00:50:54,962
When he attacked you,
did he speak to you?

780
00:50:55,020 --> 00:50:57,570
Yes. He said he wanted his letters back.

781
00:50:57,990 --> 00:51:00,459
- Did you recognise his voice?
- Yes, of course.

782
00:51:00,460 --> 00:51:03,759
- You're absolutely sure of that?
- Yes, yes, of course I am.

783
00:51:03,760 --> 00:51:05,340
Thank you, Mr Toombs.

784
00:51:10,740 --> 00:51:13,858
- See you later, Paul.
- What about that tape, Jacqui?

785
00:51:13,870 --> 00:51:15,800
I'm afraid I haven't seen it.

786
00:51:17,080 --> 00:51:20,709
Then what we're going to do
is go to the CPS offices and find it.

787
00:51:20,710 --> 00:51:21,979
Ring Elliott. Make sure he's around.

788
00:51:21,980 --> 00:51:24,440
There's another matter
we have to discuss.

789
00:51:25,580 --> 00:51:27,149
I don't understand this, Elliott.

790
00:51:27,150 --> 00:51:30,019
I can almost believe that
the CPS doesn't want us to win.

791
00:51:30,020 --> 00:51:32,189
What are you saying, Jo? We are winning.

792
00:51:32,190 --> 00:51:33,959
We jeopardised our main witness

793
00:51:33,960 --> 00:51:35,959
because he was not told
of the consequences

794
00:51:35,960 --> 00:51:38,699
- of his incriminating himself.
- It was worth the risk.

795
00:51:38,700 --> 00:51:39,700
(SCOFFS)

796
00:51:39,830 --> 00:51:43,948
The case against Roxborough is good, Jo.
You're making all your points.

797
00:51:46,040 --> 00:51:47,769
I still want to hear the tape.

798
00:51:47,770 --> 00:51:51,829
It's simply a record of radio calls
to police area cars, nothing more.

799
00:51:55,350 --> 00:51:57,527
The call at 16:55 advising the police

800
00:51:57,580 --> 00:52:00,149
to be on the lookout for a silver Alfa
driven by Roxborough is there,

801
00:52:00,150 --> 00:52:02,519
so, too, is PC Welkin's call in
to say that they'd stopped

802
00:52:02,520 --> 00:52:05,991
- and arrested the MP at 17:01.
- Everything is here, is it?

803
00:52:06,230 --> 00:52:08,289
Well, it certainly won't
aid the defence.

804
00:52:08,290 --> 00:52:11,059
- Have we disclosed the tape to them?
- Of course not.

805
00:52:11,060 --> 00:52:15,100
- Jo, get on board with us here.
- We are doing our best, Jo.

806
00:52:18,440 --> 00:52:21,009
OFFICER ON TAPE: (ECHOING)
Control to Charlie Victor Zero Nine.

807
00:52:21,010 --> 00:52:22,680
Suspect, Alan Roxborough.

808
00:52:22,980 --> 00:52:24,779
Wanted inc onnec tion with attempted...

809
00:52:24,780 --> 00:52:26,800
Driving a silver Alfa Romeo...

810
00:52:27,450 --> 00:52:29,260
(OFFICER'S VOICE ECHOING)

811
00:52:57,480 --> 00:52:58,839
ON MACHINE: This is Row Colemore.

812
00:52:58,840 --> 00:53:01,909
I can't take your call right now,
but do leave a message. Thanks.

813
00:53:01,910 --> 00:53:03,280
Row, it's Jo Mills.

814
00:53:03,550 --> 00:53:06,019
I've got the radio traffic tape
from the police car

815
00:53:06,020 --> 00:53:09,844
that stopped Alan Roxborough.
What is it I'm looking for? Call me.

816
00:53:17,830 --> 00:53:19,459
ON MACHINE: This is John Deed.
Please leave...

817
00:53:19,460 --> 00:53:20,460
(TURNS OFF PHONE)

818
00:53:24,640 --> 00:53:26,100
(INTERCOM BUZZING)

819
00:53:29,940 --> 00:53:32,050
- Yes?
- It's John Deed.

820
00:53:33,310 --> 00:53:35,770
- Is anything wrong?
- Can I come in?

821
00:53:36,950 --> 00:53:38,680
Um, yeah. Push the door.

822
00:53:39,820 --> 00:53:41,130
(DOOR BUZZING)

823
00:53:54,930 --> 00:53:57,813
- Have we got an appointment?
- You know we don't.

824
00:54:03,510 --> 00:54:07,334
- I've been thinking of you.
- I've told you, lots of men do that.

825
00:54:10,680 --> 00:54:11,680
You're my client.

826
00:54:11,820 --> 00:54:14,250
Yes. That's my excuse
for behaving badly.

827
00:54:17,160 --> 00:54:19,350
I just want to have dinner with you.

828
00:54:19,860 --> 00:54:23,860
- No. I'd have to stop seeing you.
- That's a contradiction in terms.

829
00:54:25,630 --> 00:54:26,630
Yeah.

830
00:54:29,430 --> 00:54:34,030
I think you're as lonely
and isolated as I sometimes feel.

831
00:55:07,270 --> 00:55:09,260
Oh, you cook as well, Sir John.

832
00:55:09,640 --> 00:55:12,817
Shouldn't you call me John
now you're wearing my shirt?

833
00:55:12,950 --> 00:55:16,421
If I did that, the balance
of our relationship might change.

834
00:55:16,550 --> 00:55:19,080
- I thought it had.
- There's no going back.

835
00:55:19,280 --> 00:55:20,570
Is there ever?

836
00:55:22,990 --> 00:55:23,990
CRAWCHECK: Mmm.

837
00:55:30,360 --> 00:55:33,125
Now that we've made love,
I won't see you again.

838
00:55:33,830 --> 00:55:36,125
- Not as a therapist?
- Not as anything.

839
00:55:36,200 --> 00:55:38,099
I'm not a woman that likes to be dumped.

840
00:55:38,100 --> 00:55:40,240
- Do they ever?
- You'd be surprised.

841
00:55:42,640 --> 00:55:44,660
And if I promise not to do that?

842
00:55:46,350 --> 00:55:49,527
- I'm not sure you can help yourself.
- Well, I do try.

843
00:55:50,380 --> 00:55:52,110
I did therapy, didn't I?

844
00:55:52,680 --> 00:55:55,210
Yes, and you've achieved
a lot in your life.

845
00:55:55,420 --> 00:55:58,303
But we never got
to lasting relationships, did we?

846
00:55:59,460 --> 00:56:01,130
I think you abandon women

847
00:56:01,430 --> 00:56:04,059
because somewhere inside,
there's a little boy

848
00:56:04,060 --> 00:56:06,110
who knows he can't rely on love.

849
00:56:07,070 --> 00:56:10,600
Feels unworthy of it
and can't bear to feel vulnerable again.

850
00:56:11,240 --> 00:56:13,439
Yes. I understand intellectually,
but that doesn't help.

851
00:56:13,440 --> 00:56:15,840
I can't go back
and be that 10-year-old.

852
00:56:16,510 --> 00:56:18,440
I know you feel disconnected.

853
00:56:18,910 --> 00:56:21,852
But the connection with the past
is in the present.

854
00:56:22,010 --> 00:56:24,658
You have to reach back
and integrate it, John.

855
00:56:27,490 --> 00:56:30,314
This must be getting serious.
You called me John.

856
00:56:35,590 --> 00:56:38,559
When you examined
the Burberry mackintosh, Dr Vaughn,

857
00:56:38,560 --> 00:56:40,796
can you tell the court what you found?

858
00:56:40,900 --> 00:56:42,629
Blood staining
on the back of the garment,

859
00:56:42,630 --> 00:56:45,199
three patches
roughly the size of golf balls.

860
00:56:45,200 --> 00:56:47,309
Were you able to establish
whose blood it was?

861
00:56:47,310 --> 00:56:49,480
We DNA matched it to Donald Toombs.

862
00:56:50,180 --> 00:56:54,310
Could you examine exhibit number 27,

863
00:56:55,380 --> 00:56:58,145
the iron frying pan,
and tell us what you found?

864
00:57:05,220 --> 00:57:09,515
It has blood and skin traces on it
that were a DNA match to Donald Toombs.

865
00:57:10,200 --> 00:57:13,142
There were fingerprints belonging
to the defendant.

866
00:57:16,740 --> 00:57:21,093
Were you able to establish if this
was the weapon used to attack Mr Toombs?

867
00:57:21,340 --> 00:57:22,680
Yes, clearly.

868
00:57:23,280 --> 00:57:25,986
Then it's not unreasonable
his blood was on it?

869
00:57:26,240 --> 00:57:28,200
No. It was used in the attack.

870
00:57:28,650 --> 00:57:32,400
When it wasn't being used
as a blunt instrument of mayhem,

871
00:57:33,490 --> 00:57:35,300
it was a cooking implement.

872
00:57:35,850 --> 00:57:36,850
Yes.

873
00:57:37,020 --> 00:57:40,197
So, is it surprising that Mr Toombs'
prints were on it?

874
00:57:41,630 --> 00:57:42,630
No.

875
00:57:43,830 --> 00:57:46,536
If a person had a relationship
with the victim,

876
00:57:46,700 --> 00:57:48,920
if they prepared food in the kitchen,

877
00:57:49,330 --> 00:57:52,683
would it be surprising to find that
person's prints there?

878
00:57:54,610 --> 00:57:55,728
No, it wouldn't be.

879
00:57:55,940 --> 00:57:58,570
So there was nothing
in itself significant

880
00:57:59,540 --> 00:58:01,658
in finding Mr Roxborough's prints...

881
00:58:01,680 --> 00:58:04,719
In these circumstances,
it indicates that the defendant

882
00:58:04,720 --> 00:58:07,000
used the frying pan to hit Mr Toombs.

883
00:58:07,420 --> 00:58:09,120
You're certain of that?

884
00:58:10,060 --> 00:58:12,610
No. It is a significant indication.

885
00:58:14,130 --> 00:58:15,130
Hmm.

886
00:58:15,790 --> 00:58:19,360
You gave evidence
in a murder case in 1998.

887
00:58:20,400 --> 00:58:23,106
There, you were equally sure
that a claw hammer

888
00:58:23,870 --> 00:58:26,509
bearing the fingerprints of both
the victim and the defendant

889
00:58:26,510 --> 00:58:29,669
indicated the defendant had used it
to kill his victim.

890
00:58:29,670 --> 00:58:33,729
The defendant, George Meddicot,
was in fact convicted for murder, yes?

891
00:58:33,980 --> 00:58:36,000
- Yes.
- Didn't hear that, Doctor.

892
00:58:37,620 --> 00:58:40,170
- Yes. He was.
- Yes.

893
00:58:42,590 --> 00:58:47,100
And just last month, the Court of Appeal
set that conviction aside

894
00:58:47,790 --> 00:58:49,460
because it was unsafe.

895
00:58:55,930 --> 00:58:58,210
Thank you, Doctor. You can step down.

896
00:58:59,740 --> 00:59:03,239
That was my last witness and concludes
the case for the Crown, My Lord.

897
00:59:03,240 --> 00:59:06,179
Thank you, Mrs Mills.
Mr Cantwell, am I to hear from you?

898
00:59:06,180 --> 00:59:09,780
No. I'd like to call my first witness,
Alan Roxborough, MP.

899
00:59:10,780 --> 00:59:12,090
Mr Roxborough,

900
00:59:13,220 --> 00:59:17,515
can you tell the court for how long
you had a relationship with Mr Toombs?

901
00:59:18,590 --> 00:59:21,340
We were friends for about 15 months.

902
00:59:22,660 --> 00:59:24,099
We'd met on Rotherwas Common.

903
00:59:24,100 --> 00:59:26,299
He'd caught my dog
as it was running towards the road.

904
00:59:26,300 --> 00:59:28,520
What caused the relationship to end?

905
00:59:29,030 --> 00:59:30,990
My rapprochement with my wife.

906
00:59:32,840 --> 00:59:35,120
Did Mr Toombs ever ask you for money?

907
00:59:39,480 --> 00:59:41,230
- Yes, he did.
- Much money?

908
00:59:43,620 --> 00:59:46,549
He asked me for ￡1,000.
This he increased to ￡2,000.

909
00:59:46,550 --> 00:59:48,789
Did you ever accede
to his demands for money?

910
00:59:48,790 --> 00:59:51,370
- No.
- How did his demands come about?

911
00:59:53,930 --> 00:59:56,829
Well, he said he was broke, he couldn't
work because of a skin complaint,

912
00:59:56,830 --> 00:59:57,959
he was behind with his rent.

913
00:59:57,960 --> 01:00:01,199
That's when he asked if I could
loan him ￡1,000. I said no.

914
01:00:01,200 --> 01:00:02,720
Did he threaten you?

915
01:00:04,100 --> 01:00:07,865
Well, he joked about how my wife
wouldn't like to know about him.

916
01:00:08,040 --> 01:00:09,869
I told him that she already knew.

917
01:00:09,870 --> 01:00:12,710
Did he repeat his request for money?

918
01:00:13,480 --> 01:00:15,060
Yes. Two weeks later.

919
01:00:15,950 --> 01:00:19,419
I'd mentioned that I'd been offered
a job in the government.

920
01:00:19,420 --> 01:00:22,119
It was then that I asked him
for the letters that I'd written to him.

921
01:00:22,120 --> 01:00:25,062
He said I could have them
if I gave him the ￡2,000.

922
01:00:25,360 --> 01:00:28,659
Were you worried the letters would
compromise you in government?

923
01:00:28,660 --> 01:00:29,660
No.

924
01:00:31,030 --> 01:00:32,760
They would embarrass me.

925
01:00:34,370 --> 01:00:38,606
I had said hurtful things about people
because I was feeling overwhelmed,

926
01:00:39,600 --> 01:00:43,880
impotent to further my investigation
into unexplained deaths

927
01:00:44,540 --> 01:00:46,070
in the armaments industry.

928
01:00:46,110 --> 01:00:49,379
- Did you recover any of the letters?
- Yes. He returned four,

929
01:00:49,380 --> 01:00:52,379
but I decided not to take
the government job anyway.

930
01:00:52,380 --> 01:00:56,204
Instead, I continued trying
to find answers to explain the deaths.

931
01:00:58,520 --> 01:01:00,330
Let us be clear about this.

932
01:01:01,660 --> 01:01:05,700
Did you ever give Mr Toombs
money for the letters

933
01:01:07,170 --> 01:01:09,660
or to shut him up?

934
01:01:11,900 --> 01:01:13,959
No. As God is my witness, I didn't.

935
01:01:47,410 --> 01:01:50,630
Yes. A little, please. That's it.

936
01:01:53,580 --> 01:01:56,698
DANIELS: Is Alan Roxborough gonna
be found not guilty?

937
01:01:57,680 --> 01:02:00,210
Well, I've done
just about everything I can.

938
01:02:00,550 --> 01:02:04,550
You know, doctoring a police radio
traffic tape could cost me my job,

939
01:02:04,820 --> 01:02:05,960
even my liberty.

940
01:02:06,190 --> 01:02:07,459
For the kind of money
you'll be collecting,

941
01:02:07,460 --> 01:02:10,629
- you won't even think twice about it.
- Hopefully I won't have to.

942
01:02:10,630 --> 01:02:12,180
I want the job done.

943
01:02:13,630 --> 01:02:15,530
Well, the judge is the unknown.

944
01:02:15,870 --> 01:02:18,988
Unfortunately, our judges
aren't biddable, Mr Daniels.

945
01:02:19,300 --> 01:02:22,700
In this race, Row,
no one gets the prize for being second.

946
01:02:25,140 --> 01:02:27,846
I will do what I can
to have the trial stopped.

947
01:02:27,980 --> 01:02:32,451
I don't care how. Just get that MP free
to go on attacking Whittakers' board.

948
01:02:33,490 --> 01:02:36,314
They only need one more push
for us to take over.

949
01:02:36,490 --> 01:02:38,190
Now, is that understood?

950
01:02:45,700 --> 01:02:48,759
Mr Roxborough, how much money did
you give Mr Toombs?

951
01:02:49,270 --> 01:02:50,270
None.

952
01:02:50,940 --> 01:02:52,750
I said I didn't. I didn't.

953
01:02:53,910 --> 01:02:56,570
- Not even for sex?
- No, certainly not.

954
01:02:58,280 --> 01:03:00,179
This is a homosexual
who is known to the police

955
01:03:00,180 --> 01:03:04,190
for pick-ups on Rotherwas Common.
Do you really think that this jury

956
01:03:04,820 --> 01:03:07,703
is going to believe
that you never gave him money?

957
01:03:07,750 --> 01:03:09,300
It's true. I didn't.

958
01:03:09,620 --> 01:03:12,610
Or that you never gave Mr Fedor
money for sex?

959
01:03:13,120 --> 01:03:14,380
No. I didn't.

960
01:03:16,660 --> 01:03:17,800
I didn't.

961
01:03:20,830 --> 01:03:22,769
This is your bank statement
for March of this year.

962
01:03:22,770 --> 01:03:25,460
- Item 54 in your bundle, My Lord.
- Yeah.

963
01:03:26,100 --> 01:03:28,030
Can you look on the statement

964
01:03:29,510 --> 01:03:32,805
at the cash withdrawals you made
for the 7th and the 12th

965
01:03:33,750 --> 01:03:35,449
and tell us what they're for?

966
01:03:35,450 --> 01:03:37,179
Well, I was buying my daughter a car.

967
01:03:37,180 --> 01:03:42,010
￡1,000, a week after Mr Toombs
had demanded ￡2,000 from you.

968
01:03:42,750 --> 01:03:46,260
And then another ￡1,000 five days later.

969
01:03:47,190 --> 01:03:50,249
- I didn't pay him.
- What did you do with the money?

970
01:03:51,100 --> 01:03:53,983
- I don't know.
- Did you buy your daughter a car?

971
01:03:54,770 --> 01:03:55,770
No.

972
01:03:55,870 --> 01:03:58,753
￡2,000 is a lot of cash not to know
where it went.

973
01:03:59,200 --> 01:04:01,965
Well, I kept it about
my person for expenditure.

974
01:04:02,670 --> 01:04:04,810
Well, then, look at item 54 again.

975
01:04:05,240 --> 01:04:10,130
You withdrew ￡50 the day following
the withdrawal of the first ￡1,000.

976
01:04:11,420 --> 01:04:15,479
- Yes, but that was my wife's.
- Two days later, another ￡50 went out.

977
01:04:15,750 --> 01:04:19,221
And then two days after
the withdrawal of the second ￡1,000,

978
01:04:19,320 --> 01:04:22,390
on the Saturday, you took out ￡100.

979
01:04:23,160 --> 01:04:27,337
Had you forgotten that you had this
large sum of cash about your person?

980
01:04:27,430 --> 01:04:28,999
No, no, no.
I was still looking for a car.

981
01:04:29,000 --> 01:04:32,699
Isn't it true that you gave Mr Toombs
the ￡1,000 that he demanded

982
01:04:32,700 --> 01:04:35,409
- and he gave you four of the letters?
- No, it isn't.

983
01:04:35,410 --> 01:04:37,879
Then how were letters you had written
to him in your possession?

984
01:04:37,880 --> 01:04:39,233
He returned them to me.

985
01:04:39,310 --> 01:04:42,249
Coincidentally, after he had
demanded money from you?

986
01:04:42,250 --> 01:04:44,749
Isn't it true that
in spite of what you paid him,

987
01:04:44,750 --> 01:04:47,339
he kept back the three
most damaging letters,

988
01:04:47,690 --> 01:04:51,389
- for which he demanded further payment?
- No. I didn't give him a penny.

989
01:04:51,390 --> 01:04:52,819
On Sunday the 15th of March,

990
01:04:52,820 --> 01:04:55,559
you went to Mr Toombs' apartment
to get those letters by any means,

991
01:04:55,560 --> 01:04:57,499
- did you not?
- No. I went there to tell him

992
01:04:57,500 --> 01:05:00,669
- that I wouldn't see him again.
- But why not tell him on the phone?

993
01:05:00,670 --> 01:05:03,435
Because I thought
it would be kinder to see him.

994
01:05:03,600 --> 01:05:06,590
Oh, you're a kind MP, are you?

995
01:05:07,070 --> 01:05:10,109
One who occasionally writes
unkind things about people

996
01:05:10,110 --> 01:05:12,040
he is supposed to be helping.

997
01:05:12,940 --> 01:05:14,020
Isn't it true

998
01:05:14,280 --> 01:05:16,919
that Mr Toombs said he wasn't going
to give you the three other letters

999
01:05:16,920 --> 01:05:19,549
- unless you gave him ￡1,000?
- Yes, he did say that.

1000
01:05:19,550 --> 01:05:21,950
Was that a second ￡1,000, Mr Roxborough?

1001
01:05:22,350 --> 01:05:24,340
No. There was no first payment.

1002
01:05:25,190 --> 01:05:28,760
Mr Roxborough, could you look at item 59

1003
01:05:29,830 --> 01:05:32,948
in the bundle before you
and tell the jury what it is?

1004
01:05:38,200 --> 01:05:41,909
It's a rent statement from Rotherwas
Council for Donald Toombs' flat.

1005
01:05:41,910 --> 01:05:44,910
Can you tell us what the entry is
for March the 9th?

1006
01:05:45,880 --> 01:05:50,680
Um, it's an arrears payment for ￡674.80.

1007
01:05:51,420 --> 01:05:55,370
Made two days after the
withdrawal of the first ￡1,000

1008
01:05:55,990 --> 01:06:00,049
from your account, following the demands
by your lover, Donald Toombs.

1009
01:06:00,730 --> 01:06:02,389
(SCOFFING) Is that a coincidence?

1010
01:06:02,390 --> 01:06:04,659
Well, it must be.
These things do happen.

1011
01:06:04,660 --> 01:06:08,484
And how many of the jury have
experienced such happy coincidences?

1012
01:06:08,770 --> 01:06:10,299
Are you going to ask them, Mrs Mills?

1013
01:06:10,300 --> 01:06:13,183
I think they may already
have made up their minds.

1014
01:06:15,340 --> 01:06:18,105
When Mr Toombs refused
to part with the letters,

1015
01:06:18,110 --> 01:06:20,993
did you call him,
as a neighbour said in evidence,

1016
01:06:21,250 --> 01:06:25,133
- "a filthy, blackmailing guttersnipe"?
- I expect so. I was angry.

1017
01:06:25,420 --> 01:06:28,319
Well, a filthy, blackmailing guttersnipe
did have very damaging letters

1018
01:06:28,320 --> 01:06:31,119
- that could keep you out of government.
- No. I'd decided not to take the job.

1019
01:06:31,120 --> 01:06:33,119
Because Mr Toombs was
threatening to expose you?

1020
01:06:33,120 --> 01:06:34,729
No. He had nothing
to do with my decision.

1021
01:06:34,730 --> 01:06:36,659
Then why were you so desperate
to get his letters back?

1022
01:06:36,660 --> 01:06:39,229
Because I didn't want my constituents
to think unkindly of me.

1023
01:06:39,230 --> 01:06:41,280
So much so that in a fit of panic,

1024
01:06:41,630 --> 01:06:45,866
you were prepared to bash out Donald
Toombs' brains to cover your tracks?

1025
01:06:46,070 --> 01:06:48,609
What you hadn't reckoned on
was the neighbourliness

1026
01:06:48,610 --> 01:06:50,479
in the block where Mr Toombs lived.

1027
01:06:50,480 --> 01:06:52,839
When Mrs Wheatley rang the bell
to ask if he was all right

1028
01:06:52,840 --> 01:06:55,309
and you realised that you couldn't
go through with your plans...

1029
01:06:55,310 --> 01:06:57,479
- There was no plan!
- ...you came back a little later.

1030
01:06:57,480 --> 01:07:00,489
Let yourself into the flat with a key
that you had taken

1031
01:07:00,490 --> 01:07:02,919
and attacked Mr Toombs
and took the letters,

1032
01:07:02,920 --> 01:07:07,140
only to be thwarted again
by his good neighbours.

1033
01:07:08,730 --> 01:07:12,159
That wasn't me. It makes no sense.
Why would I risk his neighbours again?

1034
01:07:12,160 --> 01:07:13,799
Does he have a point, Mrs Mills?

1035
01:07:13,800 --> 01:07:17,153
A fear of being exposed often
causes us to abandon reason.

1036
01:07:17,200 --> 01:07:18,310
I didn't!

1037
01:07:19,940 --> 01:07:21,117
You must believe me.

1038
01:07:21,340 --> 01:07:23,811
I don't have to believe you,
Mr Roxborough.

1039
01:07:24,040 --> 01:07:25,120
They do.

1040
01:07:25,740 --> 01:07:28,490
I think this is a convenient moment.

1041
01:07:29,910 --> 01:07:31,310
USHER: All rise.

1042
01:07:47,000 --> 01:07:48,589
I had an electronics expert

1043
01:07:48,830 --> 01:07:52,125
listen to the police radio traffic tape
the CPS gave you.

1044
01:07:52,270 --> 01:07:54,469
- It's doctored.
- Oh, you can't be serious.

1045
01:07:54,470 --> 01:07:57,779
He's reassembled the edits,
measuring the different echoes.

1046
01:07:57,780 --> 01:08:01,604
He estimates that the call
to arrest Roxborough went out at 16:40.

1047
01:08:02,110 --> 01:08:04,919
That's when Roxborough was
at Toombs' flat the first time,

1048
01:08:04,920 --> 01:08:07,156
- arguing about the letters.
- Yes, Jo.

1049
01:08:08,020 --> 01:08:10,726
The call from the control room
to arrest the MP

1050
01:08:10,760 --> 01:08:14,466
seems to have gone out before
he supposedly committed the crime.

1051
01:08:14,790 --> 01:08:16,600
Then there is a conspiracy.

1052
01:08:17,230 --> 01:08:18,701
(EXCLAIMS IN FRUSTRATION)

1053
01:08:20,800 --> 01:08:24,095
If I disclose this,
the prosecution's case will collapse.

1054
01:08:27,040 --> 01:08:29,230
Control to Charlie Victor Zero Nine.

1055
01:08:29,670 --> 01:08:31,340
Suspect, Alan Roxborough.

1056
01:08:31,680 --> 01:08:34,309
Wanted inc onnec tion
with attempted murder.

1057
01:08:34,310 --> 01:08:36,450
Time logged, 16:55.

1058
01:08:38,020 --> 01:08:39,190
(REWINDING)

1059
01:08:39,420 --> 01:08:41,610
Control to Charlie Victor Zero Nine.

1060
01:08:42,090 --> 01:08:43,760
Suspect, Alan Roxborough.

1061
01:08:44,090 --> 01:08:46,719
Wanted inc onnec tion
with attempted murder.

1062
01:08:46,720 --> 01:08:48,860
Time logged, 16:55.

1063
01:08:49,860 --> 01:08:51,060
(REWINDING)

1064
01:08:51,430 --> 01:08:54,270
...attempted murder. Time logged, 16:55.

1065
01:08:56,830 --> 01:08:57,910
(SIGHS)

1066
01:09:30,640 --> 01:09:33,809
ON MACHINE: This is Elliott Stearnman.
I'm not able to speak at present.

1067
01:09:33,810 --> 01:09:36,469
If you leave your name and number,
I'll ring you back. Thanks.

1068
01:09:36,470 --> 01:09:37,520
(BEEPS)

1069
01:09:38,380 --> 01:09:39,960
Elliott, it's Jo Mills.

1070
01:09:40,280 --> 01:09:41,979
I've listened to the tape
of the radio calls

1071
01:09:41,980 --> 01:09:46,039
from control to the police area cars.
I think it's been tampered with.

1072
01:09:46,350 --> 01:09:49,880
Call me soon because I'm going to
have to speak to the judge.

1073
01:09:53,960 --> 01:09:57,799
So what makes DAC Colemore convinced
this tape has been tampered with?

1074
01:09:57,800 --> 01:10:00,230
He had an electronics expert examine it.

1075
01:10:00,770 --> 01:10:02,065
Is this the only copy?

1076
01:10:02,270 --> 01:10:05,069
I've asked for the original,
nothing materialised.

1077
01:10:05,070 --> 01:10:07,120
What time was the 999 call made?

1078
01:10:07,740 --> 01:10:11,309
Well, if the expert is correct,
the call was made to the police car

1079
01:10:11,310 --> 01:10:14,300
before the MP even
committed the alleged crime.

1080
01:10:14,780 --> 01:10:17,519
At best, that's a conspiracy
to pervert the course of justice.

1081
01:10:17,520 --> 01:10:20,860
At worst, someone is conspiring
to shut Roxborough down.

1082
01:10:21,590 --> 01:10:22,990
It's a big leap.

1083
01:10:24,790 --> 01:10:28,261
I can believe that the CPS
is incompetent, the police, well,

1084
01:10:29,560 --> 01:10:32,559
we know that they sometimes fit
the evidence to the suspect,

1085
01:10:32,560 --> 01:10:35,619
but I don't believe that
they are working in concert.

1086
01:10:36,570 --> 01:10:37,710
(JO SIGHS)

1087
01:10:41,140 --> 01:10:42,905
Should I disclose what I know?

1088
01:10:43,010 --> 01:10:46,363
Are you convinced that this tape
has been interfered with?

1089
01:10:47,680 --> 01:10:50,033
Without the original,
I can't be certain.

1090
01:10:50,080 --> 01:10:52,879
Then you must go to the defence
with your suspicions

1091
01:10:52,880 --> 01:10:55,160
and ask Brian Cantwell to raise them.

1092
01:10:57,560 --> 01:11:00,266
This is the end of
our formal ex parte hearing.

1093
01:11:07,830 --> 01:11:08,830
Wow.

1094
01:11:10,230 --> 01:11:13,830
If this tape turns out to be genuine,
you're in big trouble.

1095
01:11:15,010 --> 01:11:17,422
I doubt if the CPS
would employ you again.

1096
01:11:17,510 --> 01:11:19,805
- Shouldn't think anybody will.
- Judge?

1097
01:11:20,180 --> 01:11:23,049
It is something for the defence
to put to the jury.

1098
01:11:23,050 --> 01:11:25,349
Well, perhaps I should just
give the tape to Brian Cantwell.

1099
01:11:25,350 --> 01:11:27,100
And it is career suicide.

1100
01:11:27,890 --> 01:11:29,200
This isn't just!

1101
01:11:31,490 --> 01:11:34,549
You have to show that the call
to the police car came

1102
01:11:34,730 --> 01:11:36,720
before the crime was committed.

1103
01:11:39,030 --> 01:11:40,760
How'd you get into this?

1104
01:11:41,170 --> 01:11:45,170
Row Colemore said he thought
there was something wrong with the tape.

1105
01:11:45,440 --> 01:11:49,499
- Does Row have an interest in this?
- How? He's thinking of retiring.

1106
01:11:49,840 --> 01:11:51,950
Yeah, but where's he retiring to?

1107
01:11:55,380 --> 01:11:56,900
Come on, it's late.

1108
01:11:57,450 --> 01:11:59,850
Do you want to come back
to the lodgings?

1109
01:12:02,750 --> 01:12:03,830
Are you kidding?

1110
01:12:04,090 --> 01:12:08,208
Oh, don't start all that. I'll move
one of the staff out of their room.

1111
01:12:09,760 --> 01:12:13,290
You need to put in a lot more time
with your therapist first.

1112
01:12:23,710 --> 01:12:25,110
The fact is, Jo,

1113
01:12:29,710 --> 01:12:31,140
I slept with her.

1114
01:12:42,560 --> 01:12:46,340
- Actually, she's helped me quite a lot.
- Oh, how nice for you.

1115
01:12:47,000 --> 01:12:49,250
Do you know, for one brief moment,

1116
01:12:49,630 --> 01:12:53,395
I was stupid enough to think
that you seriously wanted to change.

1117
01:12:54,240 --> 01:12:56,170
Perhaps I need psychotherapy.

1118
01:13:11,960 --> 01:13:14,259
Did Elliott talk to you?
Is he coming to see me?

1119
01:13:14,260 --> 01:13:17,459
Look, Jo, I don't know what's going on
any more than you do.

1120
01:13:17,460 --> 01:13:20,813
Then find out. It's your case.
You're the one briefing me.

1121
01:13:21,030 --> 01:13:24,795
Elliott's trying to locate another copy
of the police radio tape.

1122
01:13:24,870 --> 01:13:27,576
I don't want another copy.
I want the original.

1123
01:13:39,221 --> 01:13:41,151
Mrs Mills in court yet, Coop?

1124
01:13:41,551 --> 01:13:44,490
- I did see her earlier.
- Would you mind giving her this?

1125
01:13:44,491 --> 01:13:46,590
No, actually, I'll give it
to her before we start.

1126
01:13:46,591 --> 01:13:49,944
- Oh, it's no trouble to pop it through.
- No. I'll do it.

1127
01:13:52,000 --> 01:13:55,029
- Jo, I need to talk to you.
- Nothing to talk about.

1128
01:13:55,030 --> 01:13:58,299
I finally realised that you can't change
anyone else, only yourself.

1129
01:13:58,300 --> 01:13:59,359
I can't talk here.

1130
01:13:59,500 --> 01:14:02,409
Well, should you be talking to me
at all, Judge, without defence counsel?

1131
01:14:02,410 --> 01:14:03,410
Look.

1132
01:14:04,440 --> 01:14:06,310
This could affect your case.

1133
01:14:07,380 --> 01:14:10,204
Roxborough's mentioned,
along with Frank Daniels.

1134
01:14:10,680 --> 01:14:13,119
Daniels is expected
to take over Whittakers

1135
01:14:13,120 --> 01:14:16,119
and thereby a large proportion
of the British armaments industry.

1136
01:14:16,120 --> 01:14:17,580
- Daniels.
- Hold on.

1137
01:14:18,320 --> 01:14:21,189
Daniels stands to benefit from
Roxborough undermining

1138
01:14:21,190 --> 01:14:25,661
the current management of Whittakers.
The MP can't do that if he's in prison.

1139
01:14:26,260 --> 01:14:29,672
Frank Daniels. That's who Row Colemore's
going to work for.

1140
01:14:31,370 --> 01:14:32,547
You sure about that?

1141
01:14:32,670 --> 01:14:36,469
Your friend Row is using me to undermine
the case against Roxborough.

1142
01:14:36,470 --> 01:14:38,080
He's your friend, too.

1143
01:14:39,880 --> 01:14:43,351
You must force the CPS to get you that
original police tape.

1144
01:14:43,480 --> 01:14:45,290
You're so smart, you do it.

1145
01:14:49,950 --> 01:14:53,610
Coop, could you ask DAC Colemore
to join me at the adjournment?

1146
01:14:55,030 --> 01:14:57,854
Tell him I want to hear
the original police tape.

1147
01:14:58,560 --> 01:15:00,031
He'll know what you mean.

1148
01:15:10,470 --> 01:15:14,235
Well, the judge now wants the
original police radio traffic tape.

1149
01:15:14,380 --> 01:15:15,380
Good.

1150
01:15:16,180 --> 01:15:20,592
Just swing the one you doctored onto him
and convince him it's the original.

1151
01:15:26,790 --> 01:15:30,250
I would like to call my next witness,
Mr Michael Rudkin.

1152
01:15:31,560 --> 01:15:33,260
Mr Rudkin is a solicitor

1153
01:15:33,900 --> 01:15:37,077
who witnessed Mr Roxborough
being stopped by the police

1154
01:15:37,270 --> 01:15:38,850
on the 15th of March.

1155
01:15:41,240 --> 01:15:43,909
There was no blood on Mr Roxborough's
mac when the police stopped him.

1156
01:15:43,910 --> 01:15:46,239
How close were you
to the scene of the stop?

1157
01:15:46,240 --> 01:15:48,410
I was quite close. Five metres.

1158
01:15:48,780 --> 01:15:49,950
(SCOFFING)

1159
01:15:51,350 --> 01:15:53,819
I stopped my car because I thought
there'd been an accident.

1160
01:15:53,820 --> 01:15:56,570
Mr Rudkin, would you pause
just there, please?

1161
01:15:57,020 --> 01:16:00,390
PC Welkin. You seem very agitated.

1162
01:16:00,960 --> 01:16:02,989
Is there something
that you want to say to the court?

1163
01:16:02,990 --> 01:16:05,729
- My Lord, forgive me...
- No, no, no, Mr Cantwell.

1164
01:16:05,730 --> 01:16:06,870
PC Welkin.

1165
01:16:07,670 --> 01:16:09,399
- There was no other car, sir.
- My Lord...

1166
01:16:09,400 --> 01:16:11,299
- This man is a liar!
- ...the jury must go out.

1167
01:16:11,300 --> 01:16:14,990
The police officer is still under oath.
Constable Welkin,

1168
01:16:15,610 --> 01:16:18,905
why should the jury believe you
rather than this witness?

1169
01:16:19,210 --> 01:16:22,579
- Look at the photos of the stop, sir.
- Item three in your bundle.

1170
01:16:22,580 --> 01:16:26,179
Perhaps you'd allow me to examine
the witness, as you've recalled him.

1171
01:16:26,180 --> 01:16:28,945
I really must protest against
this blatant bias.

1172
01:16:29,020 --> 01:16:30,619
JO: I must be allowed
to examine this witness.

1173
01:16:30,620 --> 01:16:32,760
Sit down and shut up, both of you.

1174
01:16:34,860 --> 01:16:36,200
Mr Rudkin,

1175
01:16:36,460 --> 01:16:39,459
would you look at item three in your
witness bundle, please?

1176
01:16:39,460 --> 01:16:40,540
The photo.

1177
01:16:40,800 --> 01:16:44,683
And perhaps you would be good enough
to indicate where you stopped.

1178
01:16:47,740 --> 01:16:49,639
I don't think where
I stopped is on this photo.

1179
01:16:49,640 --> 01:16:52,699
Well, would it have been
to the left or to the right?

1180
01:16:53,110 --> 01:16:54,340
Uh...

1181
01:16:55,180 --> 01:16:57,400
Left, I think. Yes. Left.

1182
01:16:58,480 --> 01:17:02,186
- Did you approach the police?
- No, I didn't. I didn't like to.

1183
01:17:03,620 --> 01:17:06,170
Mr Rudkin, have you ever
appeared before me?

1184
01:17:07,730 --> 01:17:10,966
- No, sir. I'm a litigation lawyer.
- You seem familiar.

1185
01:17:14,230 --> 01:17:17,890
Hmm. Mr Roxborough,
did you see Mr Rudkin's car stop?

1186
01:17:18,470 --> 01:17:20,199
Remember, you are still under oath.

1187
01:17:20,200 --> 01:17:24,009
My Lord, I really don't think
you can conduct a trial in this fashion.

1188
01:17:24,010 --> 01:17:26,760
Who says? My overriding duty as a judge,

1189
01:17:27,240 --> 01:17:29,749
to use Roskill's words
in the Crown and List, 1996,

1190
01:17:29,750 --> 01:17:33,790
- is to secure a fair trial.
- Yes. And the jury must go out.

1191
01:17:34,420 --> 01:17:36,610
No. These are questions for the jury.

1192
01:17:36,990 --> 01:17:40,285
So, Mr Roxborough,
did you see this witness stop his car?

1193
01:17:42,830 --> 01:17:45,610
- Yes. I think so.
- Where was it he stopped?

1194
01:17:46,700 --> 01:17:50,406
Like he said, to the left.
Five metres to the left on the photo.

1195
01:17:52,300 --> 01:17:56,242
Mr Rudkin, five metres ahead
of where Mr Roxborough stopped his car?

1196
01:17:57,040 --> 01:17:58,180
- Yes.
- Yes.

1197
01:17:59,080 --> 01:18:02,379
Well, Mr Rudkin, I'm sure that
the prosecution will wish to

1198
01:18:02,380 --> 01:18:04,570
question you on this at some point,

1199
01:18:05,380 --> 01:18:09,675
but five metres to the left would have
put you in the middle of a big hole

1200
01:18:10,350 --> 01:18:12,089
where a gas main was being laid,

1201
01:18:12,090 --> 01:18:15,190
as the photo in item four
in your bundle shows.

1202
01:18:19,130 --> 01:18:20,330
Oh.

1203
01:18:21,830 --> 01:18:23,970
Well, it must have been the right.

1204
01:18:27,870 --> 01:18:28,890
I see.

1205
01:18:30,040 --> 01:18:34,335
Mr Roxborough, how many victims of
unexplained deaths did you investigate?

1206
01:18:35,050 --> 01:18:36,280
Six to date.

1207
01:18:36,780 --> 01:18:39,240
All mathematicians under 30, all Asians.

1208
01:18:39,680 --> 01:18:41,790
All supposedly committed suicide.

1209
01:18:42,790 --> 01:18:45,340
Did you develop a theory
about their deaths?

1210
01:18:45,960 --> 01:18:49,229
They were all working for Whittakers
in the nuclear arms programme.

1211
01:18:49,230 --> 01:18:51,230
Each held a lot of company secrets

1212
01:18:51,430 --> 01:18:54,842
and were considering returning
to either Pakistan or India.

1213
01:18:55,170 --> 01:18:58,112
To work on their country's
nuclear programmes, hmm?

1214
01:18:59,270 --> 01:19:01,039
Had they signed
the Official Secrets Act?

1215
01:19:01,040 --> 01:19:03,393
Everyone who works for Whittakers signs,

1216
01:19:03,640 --> 01:19:07,560
but you can't eradicate information
from your mind.

1217
01:19:08,380 --> 01:19:10,310
That's what terrified people.

1218
01:19:13,880 --> 01:19:16,400
Do you concede the possibility
that someone

1219
01:19:16,820 --> 01:19:18,990
sought to stop your investigation?

1220
01:19:19,960 --> 01:19:23,313
When you embark on conspiracy theories,
where do you stop?

1221
01:19:23,730 --> 01:19:25,529
I'm a member of the British parliament.

1222
01:19:25,530 --> 01:19:28,178
My reason for being there is
to ask questions,

1223
01:19:29,200 --> 01:19:32,220
sometimes awkward,
far-reaching questions.

1224
01:19:34,840 --> 01:19:36,510
I didn't attack Donald.

1225
01:19:37,310 --> 01:19:40,000
I was indiscreet
writing him those letters.

1226
01:19:42,150 --> 01:19:44,580
I didn't give him money
to get them back.

1227
01:19:45,680 --> 01:19:49,210
I didn't have his blood on my mac
when the police stopped me.

1228
01:19:49,790 --> 01:19:51,100
They're lying.

1229
01:19:51,720 --> 01:19:53,010
- No.
- They are.

1230
01:19:53,390 --> 01:19:57,370
Between the three of you, someone is.
And we'll find out who.

1231
01:20:05,440 --> 01:20:07,870
Just whose team
are you batting for, Row?

1232
01:20:08,340 --> 01:20:11,576
- I'm on no one's team.
- You're not on the police side.

1233
01:20:12,010 --> 01:20:13,639
The case shouldn't have been brought.

1234
01:20:13,640 --> 01:20:16,549
If it hadn't, Alan Roxborough might
have continued doing what he was doing.

1235
01:20:16,550 --> 01:20:19,279
The board of Whittakers
might not have survived.

1236
01:20:19,280 --> 01:20:21,240
Isn't that pointless speculation?

1237
01:20:21,590 --> 01:20:24,819
And if he's acquitted, he may yet
make certain that they don't.

1238
01:20:24,820 --> 01:20:26,720
Is he going to be acquitted?

1239
01:20:28,560 --> 01:20:31,208
Do you know a solicitor
called Michael Rudkin?

1240
01:20:31,660 --> 01:20:33,240
No. I don't think so.

1241
01:20:35,570 --> 01:20:37,320
I saw you talking to him.

1242
01:20:38,000 --> 01:20:42,040
- Well, I know lots of solicitors.
- Don't take me for a fool, Row!

1243
01:20:42,710 --> 01:20:44,460
You've known me too long.

1244
01:20:47,080 --> 01:20:49,190
Your putative boss

1245
01:20:50,050 --> 01:20:52,649
wants Roxborough acquitted
and you're helping him.

1246
01:20:52,650 --> 01:20:56,121
- I'm doing no such thing!
- You gonna deny knowing Daniels?

1247
01:20:59,720 --> 01:21:00,720
No.

1248
01:21:02,360 --> 01:21:04,380
I do have to retire, John.

1249
01:21:06,860 --> 01:21:09,490
It's how we retire.

1250
01:21:13,300 --> 01:21:14,530
Christ, Row.

1251
01:21:15,540 --> 01:21:18,609
Look at our society out there
and its diminishing values.

1252
01:21:18,610 --> 01:21:22,560
Reputation, our word,
it's all we have of value.

1253
01:21:26,280 --> 01:21:30,019
You'd have been better employed making
sure those unexplained deaths

1254
01:21:30,020 --> 01:21:33,330
- were thoroughly investigated.
- They were, John.

1255
01:21:34,420 --> 01:21:36,730
As much as they were ever going to be.

1256
01:21:37,390 --> 01:21:41,332
The Secret Service held dossiers
on each of the scientists who died.

1257
01:21:43,070 --> 01:21:45,835
When you sign on with Whittakers,
it's for life.

1258
01:21:46,200 --> 01:21:48,160
Is that what you're doing now?

1259
01:21:49,210 --> 01:21:51,809
Those were idealistic young men
straight from university.

1260
01:21:51,810 --> 01:21:53,479
They might have understood
Einstein's theories,

1261
01:21:53,480 --> 01:21:55,670
but not what they were getting into.

1262
01:21:57,380 --> 01:22:01,040
- I want a copy of their MI5 files.
- I can't get them.

1263
01:22:02,690 --> 01:22:04,390
I could barely get that.

1264
01:22:05,890 --> 01:22:08,380
That's the original police radio
call tape.

1265
01:22:09,760 --> 01:22:11,020
Is this real?

1266
01:22:12,330 --> 01:22:16,429
Or is it a fake to make us all think
that Roxborough was conspired against?

1267
01:22:16,430 --> 01:22:17,690
It's genuine.

1268
01:22:18,640 --> 01:22:20,070
You have my word.

1269
01:22:26,440 --> 01:22:27,670
I feel used.

1270
01:22:30,780 --> 01:22:31,980
So does Jo.

1271
01:22:35,350 --> 01:22:37,100
It is for the greater good.

1272
01:22:37,420 --> 01:22:41,230
Who decides that, Row, you?
Daniels? MI5?

1273
01:22:43,560 --> 01:22:47,972
- I hope my instincts are wrong on this.
- I'll try and get you those files.

1274
01:22:58,040 --> 01:23:02,140
Coop, would you urgently find
an electronics expert

1275
01:23:02,780 --> 01:23:05,133
and get him to examine this tape for me?

1276
01:23:05,550 --> 01:23:06,750
Yes, Judge.

1277
01:23:18,630 --> 01:23:22,799
Mrs Mills, the judge would like to
see you in chambers with Mr Cantwell.

1278
01:23:22,800 --> 01:23:24,110
What's that about?

1279
01:23:24,400 --> 01:23:27,165
Perhaps he wants to tell us
about his love life.

1280
01:23:33,040 --> 01:23:34,910
As I had asked,

1281
01:23:35,280 --> 01:23:38,790
I have been passed the original
police radio traffic tape

1282
01:23:39,720 --> 01:23:41,180
from March the 15th.

1283
01:23:41,490 --> 01:23:44,549
Is this something the defence
should have had disclosed?

1284
01:23:44,550 --> 01:23:48,021
On it, the police are ordering
the arrest of Alan Roxborough

1285
01:23:48,530 --> 01:23:50,900
before the alleged crime was committed.

1286
01:23:51,330 --> 01:23:52,500
Oh, before.

1287
01:23:53,200 --> 01:23:55,269
Well, then, the trial must be stopped
and my client acquitted.

1288
01:23:55,270 --> 01:23:58,799
Well, I think I agree with you, Brian,
it would be unsafe to continue

1289
01:23:58,800 --> 01:24:02,780
if word to arrest the MP did indeed
go out 12 minutes

1290
01:24:04,040 --> 01:24:06,335
before the alleged attack on Mr Toombs.

1291
01:24:06,680 --> 01:24:11,100
But in the interest of justice, I can't
simply direct the jury to acquit.

1292
01:24:12,680 --> 01:24:16,519
Now, if I were to order a retrial,
are the CPS likely to want to go again?

1293
01:24:16,520 --> 01:24:18,589
I would certainly try to
persuade them to that view.

1294
01:24:18,590 --> 01:24:20,590
Before they even attempt to do so,

1295
01:24:20,920 --> 01:24:24,038
then they should seriously
investigate Michael Rudkin.

1296
01:24:24,330 --> 01:24:25,629
Because I'm convinced that he was

1297
01:24:25,630 --> 01:24:28,395
parachuted in as
part of a conspiracy to acquit.

1298
01:24:28,700 --> 01:24:32,160
- Judge, that's a bit of a stretch.
- Well, is it?

1299
01:24:32,970 --> 01:24:36,609
This is being passed off as the
original police radio traffic tape.

1300
01:24:36,610 --> 01:24:39,330
It's been examined
by an electronics expert

1301
01:24:39,810 --> 01:24:41,480
and it's been doctored.

1302
01:24:42,050 --> 01:24:45,210
This is the tape that the CPS
gave to you, Jo.

1303
01:24:45,850 --> 01:24:47,340
And it's authentic.

1304
01:24:49,350 --> 01:24:52,409
I'm afraid Row Colemore
has been lying to both of us.

1305
01:24:53,120 --> 01:24:55,459
Now it's clear to me
that Alan Roxborough

1306
01:24:55,460 --> 01:24:58,637
did attack his lover,
probably to get the letters back.

1307
01:24:58,860 --> 01:25:03,069
But whether he intended to kill him,
well, now, that's a matter for a jury.

1308
01:25:03,070 --> 01:25:04,650
But not this jury

1309
01:25:05,570 --> 01:25:08,923
because of the sustained and serious
conspiracy to acquit.

1310
01:25:11,610 --> 01:25:13,540
I'm going to stop this trial.

1311
01:25:14,210 --> 01:25:17,749
I'm going to send a report to the DPP
about the two main conspirators

1312
01:25:17,750 --> 01:25:19,650
because I am determined

1313
01:25:21,420 --> 01:25:24,320
that neither Frank Daniels
nor Row Colemore

1314
01:25:25,390 --> 01:25:27,120
shall prosper from this.

1315
01:25:30,990 --> 01:25:32,859
This result brings neither credit

1316
01:25:32,860 --> 01:25:35,990
nor particular shame
to you, Mr Roxborough.

1317
01:25:37,500 --> 01:25:41,206
When someone consistently denies
a crime of which he is accused,

1318
01:25:41,440 --> 01:25:44,600
there will always be doubt
as to guilt or otherwise.

1319
01:25:45,240 --> 01:25:49,476
This, perhaps, is one of the fundamental
flaws in our adversarial system.

1320
01:25:50,580 --> 01:25:53,920
My own feeling is
that you have behaved in,

1321
01:25:54,720 --> 01:25:56,680
shall we say, a risky fashion.

1322
01:25:57,890 --> 01:26:01,189
It does little to enhance
your reputation as a politician.

1323
01:26:01,190 --> 01:26:02,770
There is one way, however,

1324
01:26:03,060 --> 01:26:06,060
in which you might recover
some of that lost ground.

1325
01:26:06,930 --> 01:26:10,410
Redouble your efforts to secure justice
for the families

1326
01:26:11,030 --> 01:26:12,810
in the unexplained deaths.

1327
01:26:14,440 --> 01:26:16,909
I hope you will now
prove courageous enough

1328
01:26:16,910 --> 01:26:18,909
to continue with that investigation.

1329
01:26:18,910 --> 01:26:20,840
For now, you're free to go.

1330
01:26:22,380 --> 01:26:23,780
USHER: All rise.

1331
01:26:38,830 --> 01:26:41,478
- Mrs Mills, My Lord.
- Thank you, Mr Johnson.

1332
01:26:42,070 --> 01:26:44,541
- Will I serve lunch now?
- Yes. Thank you.

1333
01:26:57,480 --> 01:27:00,951
- You refuse me and refuse...
- I thought this was business.

1334
01:27:01,280 --> 01:27:03,470
Yes. Let's have lunch and talk.

1335
01:27:04,490 --> 01:27:06,010
I don't need either.

1336
01:27:13,330 --> 01:27:14,799
Jo, there's something else.

1337
01:27:14,800 --> 01:27:16,399
- Talk to your therapist.
- She can't help.

1338
01:27:16,400 --> 01:27:18,269
- Not with this.
- Oh, dear, how wretched for you.

1339
01:27:18,270 --> 01:27:19,790
Shut up and listen.

1340
01:27:20,800 --> 01:27:22,809
Are you still on the Roxborough case?

1341
01:27:22,810 --> 01:27:25,439
Hopefully I'll be prosecuting him again.
The CPS are taking a view.

1342
01:27:25,440 --> 01:27:27,370
Good. These were given to me.

1343
01:27:30,510 --> 01:27:32,470
- They're MI5 files.
- Yes.

1344
01:27:32,850 --> 01:27:36,389
On each of the scientists whose deaths
Roxborough was investigating.

1345
01:27:36,390 --> 01:27:39,580
- They could get you killed.
- Now, come off it, Jo.

1346
01:27:40,120 --> 01:27:44,179
Nice, middle class people don't really
believe the state kills people.

1347
01:27:46,060 --> 01:27:47,640
How did you get them?

1348
01:27:49,470 --> 01:27:50,470
John.

1349
01:27:50,760 --> 01:27:57,460
<b>*** SRT by Ocrsub.com for MacOS ***

